window.ETEXTBOOK={"units": [{"id": "u1", "title": "导入问题", "sections": [{"id": "u1_s0", "title": "导入问题", "paras": [{"id": "u1_s0_p1", "buf": ["① Can you name some traditional Chinese festivals?"], "text": "① Can you name some traditional Chinese festivals?", "chunks": ["① Can you name some traditional Chinese festivals?"]}, {"id": "u1_s0_p2", "buf": ["② Which four traditional Chinese festivals have been designated as national public holidays?"], "text": "② Which four traditional Chinese festivals have been designated as national public holidays?", "chunks": ["② Which four traditional Chinese festivals have been designated as national public holidays?"]}, {"id": "u1_s0_p3", "buf": ["③ How does your family celebrate the Spring Festival?"], "text": "③ How does your family celebrate the Spring Festival?", "chunks": ["③ How does your family celebrate the Spring Festival?"]}, {"id": "u1_s0_p4", "buf": ["④ Can you describe any customs associated with celebrating a traditional festival in your hometown?"], "text": "④ Can you describe any customs associated with celebrating a traditional festival in your hometown?", "chunks": ["④ Can you describe any customs associated with celebrating a traditional festival in your hometown?"]}]}]}, {"id": "u2", "title": "主要传统节日", "sections": [{"id": "u2_s1", "title": "春节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s1_p1", "buf": ["The Spring Festival, also called the Chinese New Year, is China's most important traditional festival, falling on the first day of the first lunar month each year. It is a time for people to return home from all over the country to celebrate together."], "text": "The Spring Festival, also called the Chinese New Year, is China's most important traditional festival, falling on the first day of the first lunar month each year. It is a time for people to return home from all over the country to celebrate together.", "chunks": ["The Spring Festival, also called the Chinese New Year, is China's most important traditional festival, falling on the first day of the first lunar month each year. It is a time for people to return home from all over the country to celebrate together."]}, {"id": "u2_s1_p2", "buf": ["The Spring Festival activities start in the early days of the 12th lunar month and continue until about the middle of the first lunar month of the following year. The Chinese government designates four days as the official public holiday for the Spring Festival—the eve of the festival and the following three days."], "text": "The Spring Festival activities start in the early days of the 12th lunar month and continue until about the middle of the first lunar month of the following year. The Chinese government designates four days as the official public holiday for the Spring Festival—the eve of the festival and the following three days.", "chunks": ["The Spring Festival activities start in the early days of the 12th lunar month and continue until about the middle of the first lunar month of the following year. The Chinese government designates four days as the official public holiday for the Spring Festival—the eve of the festival and the following three days."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s2", "title": "春节的传说", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s2_p1", "buf": ["In China, celebrating the Spring Festival is often referred to as guo nian, which literally means \"passing the year\". It symbolises bidding farewell to the old year and welcoming the new one. There are many legends about the origin of nian. According to one of the most famous legends, in ancient China, there once lived a ferocious (凶猛的) monster named \"Nian\". It lived deep at the bottom of the sea all year round and came ashore only on New Year's Eve to devour livestock and people. To escape its attack, villagers would flee to remote mountains."], "text": "In China, celebrating the Spring Festival is often referred to as guo nian, which literally means \"passing the year\". It symbolises bidding farewell to the old year and welcoming the new one. There are many legends about the origin of nian. According to one of the most famous legends, in ancient China, there once lived a ferocious (凶猛的) monster named \"Nian\". It lived deep at the bottom of the sea all year round and came ashore only on New Year's Eve to devour livestock and people. To escape its attack, villagers would flee to remote mountains.", "chunks": ["In China, celebrating the Spring Festival is often referred to as guo nian, which literally means \"passing the year\". It symbolises bidding farewell to the old year and welcoming the new one. There are many legends about the origin of nian. According to one of the most famous legends, in ancient China, there once lived a ferocious (凶猛的) monster named \"Nian\". It lived deep at the bottom of the sea all year round and came ashore only on New Year's Eve to devour livestock and people. To escape its attack, villagers would flee to remote mountains."]}, {"id": "u2_s2_p2", "buf": ["One New Year's Eve, as the villagers were preparing to flee as usual, an old beggar arrived. A grandmother living at the edge of the village gave the old man some food and advised him to flee to the mountains to avoid the monster Nian. However, the old man stroked (轻抚) his beard and said with a smile, \"If you allow me to stay at your home for the night, I'm sure I can drive away the monster Nian.\" The grandmother went on urging him to escape, but he only smiled without replying. Eventually, unable to overcome her fear, she fled to the mountains."], "text": "One New Year's Eve, as the villagers were preparing to flee as usual, an old beggar arrived. A grandmother living at the edge of the village gave the old man some food and advised him to flee to the mountains to avoid the monster Nian. However, the old man stroked (轻抚) his beard and said with a smile, \"If you allow me to stay at your home for the night, I'm sure I can drive away the monster Nian.\" The grandmother went on urging him to escape, but he only smiled without replying. Eventually, unable to overcome her fear, she fled to the mountains.", "chunks": ["One New Year's Eve, as the villagers were preparing to flee as usual, an old beggar arrived. A grandmother living at the edge of the village gave the old man some food and advised him to flee to the mountains to avoid the monster Nian. However, the old man stroked (轻抚) his beard and said with a smile, \"If you allow me to stay at your home for the night, I'm sure I can drive away the monster Nian.\" The grandmother went on urging him to escape, but he only smiled without replying. Eventually, unable to overcome her fear, she fled to the mountains."]}, {"id": "u2_s2_p3", "buf": ["Around midnight, the monster Nian rushed into the village. He found the atmosphere quite different from that of previous years. The grandmother's house at the edge of the village was brightly lit, with bright red paper pasted on the doors. Shocked, the monster gave a strange, loud cry and stared angrily at the house. As he approached the door, the sudden explosive sounds of firecrackers rang out. Trembling all over, the monster dared not take another step forward."], "text": "Around midnight, the monster Nian rushed into the village. He found the atmosphere quite different from that of previous years. The grandmother's house at the edge of the village was brightly lit, with bright red paper pasted on the doors. Shocked, the monster gave a strange, loud cry and stared angrily at the house. As he approached the door, the sudden explosive sounds of firecrackers rang out. Trembling all over, the monster dared not take another step forward.", "chunks": ["Around midnight, the monster Nian rushed into the village. He found the atmosphere quite different from that of previous years. The grandmother's house at the edge of the village was brightly lit, with bright red paper pasted on the doors. Shocked, the monster gave a strange, loud cry and stared angrily at the house. As he approached the door, the sudden explosive sounds of firecrackers rang out. Trembling all over, the monster dared not take another step forward."]}, {"id": "u2_s2_p4", "buf": ["It turned out that the colour red, bright light, and explosive sounds were what Nian feared most. When the door of the grandmother's house was flung open, and an old man in a red robe burst out laughing in the courtyard, the monster Nian was scared out of his wits and fled."], "text": "It turned out that the colour red, bright light, and explosive sounds were what Nian feared most. When the door of the grandmother's house was flung open, and an old man in a red robe burst out laughing in the courtyard, the monster Nian was scared out of his wits and fled.", "chunks": ["It turned out that the colour red, bright light, and explosive sounds were what Nian feared most. When the door of the grandmother's house was flung open, and an old man in a red robe burst out laughing in the courtyard, the monster Nian was scared out of his wits and fled."]}, {"id": "u2_s2_p5", "buf": ["From then on, on every New Year's Eve, families paste Spring Festival couplets (春联) on their doors, set off firecrackers, keep the lights on, and stay up late to keep Nian away. The next day, which is also the first day of the new lunar year, they would visit relatives and friends early in the morning to send their regards and congratulations. As time passed, these customs spread far and wide and have been passed down through the generations."], "text": "From then on, on every New Year's Eve, families paste Spring Festival couplets (春联) on their doors, set off firecrackers, keep the lights on, and stay up late to keep Nian away. The next day, which is also the first day of the new lunar year, they would visit relatives and friends early in the morning to send their regards and congratulations. As time passed, these customs spread far and wide and have been passed down through the generations.", "chunks": ["From then on, on every New Year's Eve, families paste Spring Festival couplets (春联) on their doors, set off firecrackers, keep the lights on, and stay up late to keep Nian away. The next day, which is also the first day of the new lunar year, they would visit relatives and friends early in the morning to send their regards and congratulations. As time passed, these customs spread far and wide and have been passed down through the generations."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s3", "title": "春节的活动", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s3_p1", "buf": ["In northern China, there is a saying: \"Right after laba, the new year is coming.\" The Laba Festival is generally considered the commencement of the Chinese New Year celebrations. It falls on the eighth day of the twelfth month of the lunar calendar. La (腊) refers to the twelfth lunar month, and ba (八) means \"eight(h)\" in Chinese, which is how the name is derived. People eat laba porridge on the day to give thanks for a good harvest and to wish for good luck and health in the coming year."], "text": "In northern China, there is a saying: \"Right after laba, the new year is coming.\" The Laba Festival is generally considered the commencement of the Chinese New Year celebrations. It falls on the eighth day of the twelfth month of the lunar calendar. La (腊) refers to the twelfth lunar month, and ba (八) means \"eight(h)\" in Chinese, which is how the name is derived. People eat laba porridge on the day to give thanks for a good harvest and to wish for good luck and health in the coming year.", "chunks": ["In northern China, there is a saying: \"Right after laba, the new year is coming.\" The Laba Festival is generally considered the commencement of the Chinese New Year celebrations. It falls on the eighth day of the twelfth month of the lunar calendar. La (腊) refers to the twelfth lunar month, and ba (八) means \"eight(h)\" in Chinese, which is how the name is derived. People eat laba porridge on the day to give thanks for a good harvest and to wish for good luck and health in the coming year."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p2", "buf": ["The timing of the Xiaonian Festival varies across China. In northern regions, it falls on the 23rd day of the 12th lunar month, while in southern China, it is celebrated on the 24th day. The Xiaonian Festival marks the beginning of preparations for the Spring Festival. On this day, families make offerings, such as sweet foods, to the Kitchen God."], "text": "The timing of the Xiaonian Festival varies across China. In northern regions, it falls on the 23rd day of the 12th lunar month, while in southern China, it is celebrated on the 24th day. The Xiaonian Festival marks the beginning of preparations for the Spring Festival. On this day, families make offerings, such as sweet foods, to the Kitchen God.", "chunks": ["The timing of the Xiaonian Festival varies across China. In northern regions, it falls on the 23rd day of the 12th lunar month, while in southern China, it is celebrated on the 24th day. The Xiaonian Festival marks the beginning of preparations for the Spring Festival. On this day, families make offerings, such as sweet foods, to the Kitchen God."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p3", "buf": ["Before the New Year, people thoroughly clean their homes, both inside and outside, as well as their clothes, bedding, and utensils."], "text": "Before the New Year, people thoroughly clean their homes, both inside and outside, as well as their clothes, bedding, and utensils.", "chunks": ["Before the New Year, people thoroughly clean their homes, both inside and outside, as well as their clothes, bedding, and utensils."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p4", "buf": ["Decorations are an important feature of the Spring Festival celebrations. Traditional Chinese paper cuttings are pasted on windows. The paper used is usually red, and the designs may include birds, flowers, fish, and scenic patterns. On their front doors, families put up poetic couplets written in calligraphy, symbolising life's renewal and the return of spring. In addition to putting up couplets, it is common to hang calligraphic writings of the Chinese characters 春 (spring), 财 (wealth), and 福 (blessing)."], "text": "Decorations are an important feature of the Spring Festival celebrations. Traditional Chinese paper cuttings are pasted on windows. The paper used is usually red, and the designs may include birds, flowers, fish, and scenic patterns. On their front doors, families put up poetic couplets written in calligraphy, symbolising life's renewal and the return of spring. In addition to putting up couplets, it is common to hang calligraphic writings of the Chinese characters 春 (spring), 财 (wealth), and 福 (blessing).", "chunks": ["Decorations are an important feature of the Spring Festival celebrations. Traditional Chinese paper cuttings are pasted on windows. The paper used is usually red, and the designs may include birds, flowers, fish, and scenic patterns. On their front doors, families put up poetic couplets written in calligraphy, symbolising life's renewal and the return of spring. In addition to putting up couplets, it is common to hang calligraphic writings of the Chinese characters 春 (spring), 财 (wealth), and 福 (blessing)."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p5", "buf": ["Houses are brightly lit, and a sumptuous (丰盛的) family dinner is served on Spring Festival Eve. Dishes such as chicken, fish, and tofu are traditionally included, as their pronunciations are similar to the Chinese characters 吉 (jí), 余 (yú), and 福 (fú), which mean \"auspicious\", \"abundance\", and \"blessing\", respectively. In southern China, people eat niangao (New Year cake made of glutinous rice flour), which symbolises the wish to rise \"higher and higher, year after year\". In northern China, jiaozi (Chinese dumplings) are preferred as they are believed to represent bidding farewell to the old and welcoming the new. This belief comes from the homophony between jiaozi and the Chinese phrase 更岁交子 (change of year), which signifies the transition from the old year to the new. The shape of jiaozi resembles the ancient Chinese ingot (元宝), so eating jiaozi also expresses wishes for wealth and prosperity."], "text": "Houses are brightly lit, and a sumptuous (丰盛的) family dinner is served on Spring Festival Eve. Dishes such as chicken, fish, and tofu are traditionally included, as their pronunciations are similar to the Chinese characters 吉 (jí), 余 (yú), and 福 (fú), which mean \"auspicious\", \"abundance\", and \"blessing\", respectively. In southern China, people eat niangao (New Year cake made of glutinous rice flour), which symbolises the wish to rise \"higher and higher, year after year\". In northern China, jiaozi (Chinese dumplings) are preferred as they are believed to represent bidding farewell to the old and welcoming the new. This belief comes from the homophony between jiaozi and the Chinese phrase 更岁交子 (change of year), which signifies the transition from the old year to the new. The shape of jiaozi resembles the ancient Chinese ingot (元宝), so eating jiaozi also expresses wishes for wealth and prosperity.", "chunks": ["Houses are brightly lit, and a sumptuous (丰盛的) family dinner is served on Spring Festival Eve. Dishes such as chicken, fish, and tofu are traditionally included, as their pronunciations are similar to the Chinese characters 吉 (jí), 余 (yú), and 福 (fú), which mean \"auspicious\", \"abundance\", and \"blessing\", respectively. In southern China, people eat niangao (New Year cake made of glutinous rice flour), which symbolises the wish to rise \"higher and higher, year after year\". In northern China, jiaozi (Chinese dumplings) are preferred as they are believed to represent bidding farewell to the old and welcoming the new. This belief comes from the homophony between jiaozi and the Chinese phrase 更岁交子 (change of year), which signifies the transition from the old year to the new. The shape of jiaozi resembles the ancient Chinese ingot (元宝), so eating jiaozi also expresses wishes for wealth and prosperity."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p6", "buf": ["After dinner on New Year's Eve, the whole family usually get together to chat and watch TV. Since 1983, watching the Spring Festival Gala has become an integral part of Chinese New Year celebrations for Chinese people both at home and abroad."], "text": "After dinner on New Year's Eve, the whole family usually get together to chat and watch TV. Since 1983, watching the Spring Festival Gala has become an integral part of Chinese New Year celebrations for Chinese people both at home and abroad.", "chunks": ["After dinner on New Year's Eve, the whole family usually get together to chat and watch TV. Since 1983, watching the Spring Festival Gala has become an integral part of Chinese New Year celebrations for Chinese people both at home and abroad."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p7", "buf": ["On New Year's Eve, the younger generation extends greetings to their parents and other senior family members. Parents and the elders usually wrap brand-new banknotes with red paper or place them in red envelopes and give them to the younger generation as \"lucky money\". Red symbolises vitality, joy, and good fortune. This tradition is believed to bring blessings of health and growth to children in the coming year."], "text": "On New Year's Eve, the younger generation extends greetings to their parents and other senior family members. Parents and the elders usually wrap brand-new banknotes with red paper or place them in red envelopes and give them to the younger generation as \"lucky money\". Red symbolises vitality, joy, and good fortune. This tradition is believed to bring blessings of health and growth to children in the coming year.", "chunks": ["On New Year's Eve, the younger generation extends greetings to their parents and other senior family members. Parents and the elders usually wrap brand-new banknotes with red paper or place them in red envelopes and give them to the younger generation as \"lucky money\". Red symbolises vitality, joy, and good fortune. This tradition is believed to bring blessings of health and growth to children in the coming year."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p8", "buf": ["On the morning of New Year's Day, going from door to door to wish people a Happy New Year is a fond childhood memory for many, and for children, the highlight is receiving red envelopes. Nowadays, with the rise of the internet, people are changing their ways of celebrating the Spring Festival, and red envelopes have entered the digital age. During the Chinese New Year, sending digital red envelopes has become a fashionable way to share joy and happiness with families."], "text": "On the morning of New Year's Day, going from door to door to wish people a Happy New Year is a fond childhood memory for many, and for children, the highlight is receiving red envelopes. Nowadays, with the rise of the internet, people are changing their ways of celebrating the Spring Festival, and red envelopes have entered the digital age. During the Chinese New Year, sending digital red envelopes has become a fashionable way to share joy and happiness with families.", "chunks": ["On the morning of New Year's Day, going from door to door to wish people a Happy New Year is a fond childhood memory for many, and for children, the highlight is receiving red envelopes. Nowadays, with the rise of the internet, people are changing their ways of celebrating the Spring Festival, and red envelopes have entered the digital age. During the Chinese New Year, sending digital red envelopes has become a fashionable way to share joy and happiness with families."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p9", "buf": ["Whether traditional or digital, red envelopes are among the most popular gifts during the Spring Festival. The different ways of celebrating the Chinese New Year may have evolved with the times, but its central themes—family reunion and wishes for happiness—remain unchanged."], "text": "Whether traditional or digital, red envelopes are among the most popular gifts during the Spring Festival. The different ways of celebrating the Chinese New Year may have evolved with the times, but its central themes—family reunion and wishes for happiness—remain unchanged.", "chunks": ["Whether traditional or digital, red envelopes are among the most popular gifts during the Spring Festival. The different ways of celebrating the Chinese New Year may have evolved with the times, but its central themes—family reunion and wishes for happiness—remain unchanged."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p10", "buf": ["Setting off firecrackers used to be the most typical custom during the Spring Festival because people believed that the sound would drive away evil spirits. Today, this practice is strictly regulated or banned in some cities due to security and pollution concerns. In response, more and more people are using electronic fireworks, which simulate the crackling noises and colourful bursts of real fireworks. Electronic fireworks can not only add a festive atmosphere but also help prevent dust, paper scraps, and other pollutants."], "text": "Setting off firecrackers used to be the most typical custom during the Spring Festival because people believed that the sound would drive away evil spirits. Today, this practice is strictly regulated or banned in some cities due to security and pollution concerns. In response, more and more people are using electronic fireworks, which simulate the crackling noises and colourful bursts of real fireworks. Electronic fireworks can not only add a festive atmosphere but also help prevent dust, paper scraps, and other pollutants.", "chunks": ["Setting off firecrackers used to be the most typical custom during the Spring Festival because people believed that the sound would drive away evil spirits. Today, this practice is strictly regulated or banned in some cities due to security and pollution concerns. In response, more and more people are using electronic fireworks, which simulate the crackling noises and colourful bursts of real fireworks. Electronic fireworks can not only add a festive atmosphere but also help prevent dust, paper scraps, and other pollutants."]}, {"id": "u2_s3_p11", "buf": ["The lively Spring Festival atmosphere not only fills homes but also spreads throughout streets and lanes. Activities such as lion dances, dragon dances, and temple fairs are held for days. Traditionally, the Spring Festival concludes with the Lantern Festival."], "text": "The lively Spring Festival atmosphere not only fills homes but also spreads throughout streets and lanes. Activities such as lion dances, dragon dances, and temple fairs are held for days. Traditionally, the Spring Festival concludes with the Lantern Festival.", "chunks": ["The lively Spring Festival atmosphere not only fills homes but also spreads throughout streets and lanes. Activities such as lion dances, dragon dances, and temple fairs are held for days. Traditionally, the Spring Festival concludes with the Lantern Festival."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s4", "title": "元宵节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s4_p1", "buf": ["The Lantern Festival, celebrated on the fifteenth day of the first lunar month, is closely related to the Spring Festival. It marks the end of the New Year celebrations, after which people return to their normal routines."], "text": "The Lantern Festival, celebrated on the fifteenth day of the first lunar month, is closely related to the Spring Festival. It marks the end of the New Year celebrations, after which people return to their normal routines.", "chunks": ["The Lantern Festival, celebrated on the fifteenth day of the first lunar month, is closely related to the Spring Festival. It marks the end of the New Year celebrations, after which people return to their normal routines."]}, {"id": "u2_s4_p2", "buf": ["The most prominent activity of the Lantern Festival is the grand display of beautiful lanterns. In cold regions, especially the northeastern part of China, people enjoy making ice lanterns."], "text": "The most prominent activity of the Lantern Festival is the grand display of beautiful lanterns. In cold regions, especially the northeastern part of China, people enjoy making ice lanterns.", "chunks": ["The most prominent activity of the Lantern Festival is the grand display of beautiful lanterns. In cold regions, especially the northeastern part of China, people enjoy making ice lanterns."]}, {"id": "u2_s4_p3", "buf": ["Lanterns of various shapes and sizes are hung in the streets, attracting countless visitors. Excited children hold lanterns as they stroll (闲逛) along the streets. Guessing riddles pasted onto lanterns is a tradition from the Song Dynasty. Visitors who solve a lantern riddle may receive a prize."], "text": "Lanterns of various shapes and sizes are hung in the streets, attracting countless visitors. Excited children hold lanterns as they stroll (闲逛) along the streets. Guessing riddles pasted onto lanterns is a tradition from the Song Dynasty. Visitors who solve a lantern riddle may receive a prize.", "chunks": ["Lanterns of various shapes and sizes are hung in the streets, attracting countless visitors. Excited children hold lanterns as they stroll (闲逛) along the streets. Guessing riddles pasted onto lanterns is a tradition from the Song Dynasty. Visitors who solve a lantern riddle may receive a prize."]}, {"id": "u2_s4_p4", "buf": ["Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also known by many as the Yuanxiao Festival. The round shape of yuanxiao or tangyuan is associated with reunion and being together, so people eat them to symbolise family reunion, harmony, and happiness for the year ahead. Yuanxiao or tangyuan are small dumpling balls made of glutinous rice flour with sesame (芝麻), bean paste (豆沙), date paste (枣泥), walnuts (核桃仁), or dried fruit as typical fillings. They can be boiled, fried, or steamed."], "text": "Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also known by many as the Yuanxiao Festival. The round shape of yuanxiao or tangyuan is associated with reunion and being together, so people eat them to symbolise family reunion, harmony, and happiness for the year ahead. Yuanxiao or tangyuan are small dumpling balls made of glutinous rice flour with sesame (芝麻), bean paste (豆沙), date paste (枣泥), walnuts (核桃仁), or dried fruit as typical fillings. They can be boiled, fried, or steamed.", "chunks": ["Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also known by many as the Yuanxiao Festival. The round shape of yuanxiao or tangyuan is associated with reunion and being together, so people eat them to symbolise family reunion, harmony, and happiness for the year ahead. Yuanxiao or tangyuan are small dumpling balls made of glutinous rice flour with sesame (芝麻), bean paste (豆沙), date paste (枣泥), walnuts (核桃仁), or dried fruit as typical fillings. They can be boiled, fried, or steamed."]}, {"id": "u2_s4_p5", "buf": ["the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology. These terms provide a time frame for farming. In 2016, the Twenty-Four Solar Terms were inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. The Twenty-Four Solar Terms include Beginning of Spring, Rain Water, Awakening of Insects, Spring Equinox, Pure Brightness, Grain Rain, Beginning of Summer, Grain Buds, Grain in Ear, Summer Solstice, Minor Heat, Major Heat, Beginning of Autumn, End of Heat, White Dew, Autumn Equinox, Cold Dew, Frost's Descent, Beginning of Winter, Minor Snow, Major Snow, Winter Solstice, Minor Cold, and Major Cold. 二十四节气，是基于对太阳年度运动的观察，以及对季节、气候和物候变化的认知而形成的知识体系和社会实践。二十四节气为农业生产提供了时间框架，于2016年被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。二十四节气指立春、雨水、惊蛰、春分、清明、谷雨、立夏、小满、芒种、夏至、小暑、大暑、立秋、处暑、白露、秋分、寒露、霜降、立冬、小雪、大雪、冬至、小寒、大寒。"], "text": "the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology. These terms provide a time frame for farming. In 2016, the Twenty-Four Solar Terms were inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. The Twenty-Four Solar Terms include Beginning of Spring, Rain Water, Awakening of Insects, Spring Equinox, Pure Brightness, Grain Rain, Beginning of Summer, Grain Buds, Grain in Ear, Summer Solstice, Minor Heat, Major Heat, Beginning of Autumn, End of Heat, White Dew, Autumn Equinox, Cold Dew, Frost's Descent, Beginning of Winter, Minor Snow, Major Snow, Winter Solstice, Minor Cold, and Major Cold. 二十四节气，是基于对太阳年度运动的观察，以及对季节、气候和物候变化的认知而形成的知识体系和社会实践。二十四节气为农业生产提供了时间框架，于2016年被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。二十四节气指立春、雨水、惊蛰、春分、清明、谷雨、立夏、小满、芒种、夏至、小暑、大暑、立秋、处暑、白露、秋分、寒露、霜降、立冬、小雪、大雪、冬至、小寒、大寒。", "chunks": ["the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology. These terms provide a time frame for farming. In 2016, the Twenty-Four Solar Terms were inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. The Twenty-Four Solar Terms include Beginning of Spring, Rain Water, Awakening of Insects, Spring Equinox, Pure Brightness, Grain Rain, Beginning of Summer, Grain Buds, Grain in Ear, Summer Solstice, Minor Heat, Major Heat, Beginning of Autumn, End of Heat, White Dew, Autumn Equinox, Cold Dew, Frost's Descent, Beginning of Winter, Minor Snow, Major Snow, Winter Solstice, Minor Cold, and Major Cold. 二十四节气，是基于对太阳年度运动的观察，以及对季节、气候和物候变化的认知而形成的知识体系和社会实践。二十四节气为农业生产提供了时间框架，于2016年被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。二十四节气指立春、雨水、惊蛰、春分、清明、谷雨、立夏、小满、芒种、夏至、小暑、大暑、立秋、处暑、白露、秋分、寒露、霜降、立冬、小雪、大雪、冬至、小寒、大寒。"]}]}, {"id": "u2_s5", "title": "清明节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s5_p1", "buf": ["The Qingming Festival, also known as Tomb-Sweeping Day, is a day for mourning (哀悼) the dead and honouring ancestors. It usually falls between 4th April and 6th April, the first day of the Pure Brightness solar term (清明节气), which is one of the Twenty-Four Solar Terms in the traditional Chinese calendar. Around the festival, temperatures rise and rainfall increases, signalling the start of spring ploughing (春耕) and sowing."], "text": "The Qingming Festival, also known as Tomb-Sweeping Day, is a day for mourning (哀悼) the dead and honouring ancestors. It usually falls between 4th April and 6th April, the first day of the Pure Brightness solar term (清明节气), which is one of the Twenty-Four Solar Terms in the traditional Chinese calendar. Around the festival, temperatures rise and rainfall increases, signalling the start of spring ploughing (春耕) and sowing.", "chunks": ["The Qingming Festival, also known as Tomb-Sweeping Day, is a day for mourning (哀悼) the dead and honouring ancestors. It usually falls between 4th April and 6th April, the first day of the Pure Brightness solar term (清明节气), which is one of the Twenty-Four Solar Terms in the traditional Chinese calendar. Around the festival, temperatures rise and rainfall increases, signalling the start of spring ploughing (春耕) and sowing."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s6", "title": "寒食节与清明节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s6_p1", "buf": ["The Hanshi Festival, or Cold Food Day (寒食节), is observed one or two days before the Qingming Festival. On this day, people traditionally refrain from using fire and eat only cold food. The custom commemorates Jie Zitui (介子推), a loyal official who served Prince Chong'er (重耳) of the Spring and Autumn Period. According to legend, Jie Zitui once cut flesh from his own leg to feed his exiled lord. After the lord became duke, he attempted to burn the mountain to compel Jie Zitui to return to court; Jie chose to remain with his mother and died in the flames. To mourn his sacrifice, the duke decreed that fires be forbidden on this day, and it became the Hanshi Festival."], "text": "The Hanshi Festival, or Cold Food Day (寒食节), is observed one or two days before the Qingming Festival. On this day, people traditionally refrain from using fire and eat only cold food. The custom commemorates Jie Zitui (介子推), a loyal official who served Prince Chong'er (重耳) of the Spring and Autumn Period. According to legend, Jie Zitui once cut flesh from his own leg to feed his exiled lord. After the lord became duke, he attempted to burn the mountain to compel Jie Zitui to return to court; Jie chose to remain with his mother and died in the flames. To mourn his sacrifice, the duke decreed that fires be forbidden on this day, and it became the Hanshi Festival.", "chunks": ["The Hanshi Festival, or Cold Food Day (寒食节), is observed one or two days before the Qingming Festival. On this day, people traditionally refrain from using fire and eat only cold food. The custom commemorates Jie Zitui (介子推), a loyal official who served Prince Chong'er (重耳) of the Spring and Autumn Period. According to legend, Jie Zitui once cut flesh from his own leg to feed his exiled lord. After the lord became duke, he attempted to burn the mountain to compel Jie Zitui to return to court; Jie chose to remain with his mother and died in the flames. To mourn his sacrifice, the duke decreed that fires be forbidden on this day, and it became the Hanshi Festival."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s7", "title": "清明习俗", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s7_p1", "buf": ["One of the major customs of the Qingming Festival is tomb sweeping. On this day, people commemorate and honour the dead by cleaning graves and, in traditional practice, by burning incense (祭祀时用的香) and joss paper (纸钱) and offering food, wine, and other items. Over time, this custom has been gradually replaced with more environmentally friendly practices. Some lay flowers in memory of their loved ones, while those who cannot visit cemeteries participate in online tomb-sweeping services."], "text": "One of the major customs of the Qingming Festival is tomb sweeping. On this day, people commemorate and honour the dead by cleaning graves and, in traditional practice, by burning incense (祭祀时用的香) and joss paper (纸钱) and offering food, wine, and other items. Over time, this custom has been gradually replaced with more environmentally friendly practices. Some lay flowers in memory of their loved ones, while those who cannot visit cemeteries participate in online tomb-sweeping services.", "chunks": ["One of the major customs of the Qingming Festival is tomb sweeping. On this day, people commemorate and honour the dead by cleaning graves and, in traditional practice, by burning incense (祭祀时用的香) and joss paper (纸钱) and offering food, wine, and other items. Over time, this custom has been gradually replaced with more environmentally friendly practices. Some lay flowers in memory of their loved ones, while those who cannot visit cemeteries participate in online tomb-sweeping services."]}, {"id": "u2_s7_p2", "buf": ["The Qingming Festival is also an excellent time for a spring outing as the weather warms up and nature comes to life. Activities such as playing outdoor games and flying kites are particularly popular among children. Other activities include planting trees and placing willow branches at gates, a custom believed to ward off evil and symbolise vitality."], "text": "The Qingming Festival is also an excellent time for a spring outing as the weather warms up and nature comes to life. Activities such as playing outdoor games and flying kites are particularly popular among children. Other activities include planting trees and placing willow branches at gates, a custom believed to ward off evil and symbolise vitality.", "chunks": ["The Qingming Festival is also an excellent time for a spring outing as the weather warms up and nature comes to life. Activities such as playing outdoor games and flying kites are particularly popular among children. Other activities include planting trees and placing willow branches at gates, a custom believed to ward off evil and symbolise vitality."]}, {"id": "u2_s7_p3", "buf": ["In southern China, people typically eat qingtuan, which are made with glutinous rice flour mixed with the juice of Chinese mugwort (艾草) or barley grass (大麦草). In northern China, people eat cold foods such as cold porridge and cold pancakes."], "text": "In southern China, people typically eat qingtuan, which are made with glutinous rice flour mixed with the juice of Chinese mugwort (艾草) or barley grass (大麦草). In northern China, people eat cold foods such as cold porridge and cold pancakes.", "chunks": ["In southern China, people typically eat qingtuan, which are made with glutinous rice flour mixed with the juice of Chinese mugwort (艾草) or barley grass (大麦草). In northern China, people eat cold foods such as cold porridge and cold pancakes."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s8", "title": "端午节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s8_p1", "buf": ["The Dragon Boat Festival, also known as the Duanwu Festival, falls on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month and is celebrated throughout China. There are several legends describing the origins of the festival, the most popular one being that it is in memory of Qu Yuan."], "text": "The Dragon Boat Festival, also known as the Duanwu Festival, falls on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month and is celebrated throughout China. There are several legends describing the origins of the festival, the most popular one being that it is in memory of Qu Yuan.", "chunks": ["The Dragon Boat Festival, also known as the Duanwu Festival, falls on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month and is celebrated throughout China. There are several legends describing the origins of the festival, the most popular one being that it is in memory of Qu Yuan."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s9", "title": "屈原与端午节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s9_p1", "buf": ["Qu Yuan was a minister of the State of Chu during the Warring States Period and is recognised as China's first great patriotic poet. In the face of great pressure from the powerful State of Qin, he advocated enriching the country and strengthening its military. However, his reform ideas were opposed by the aristocrats, and he was later deposed (免职) by King Huai and exiled by King Qingxiang. Even in exile, he still cared much for his country and people, and expressed these feelings in his poems."], "text": "Qu Yuan was a minister of the State of Chu during the Warring States Period and is recognised as China's first great patriotic poet. In the face of great pressure from the powerful State of Qin, he advocated enriching the country and strengthening its military. However, his reform ideas were opposed by the aristocrats, and he was later deposed (免职) by King Huai and exiled by King Qingxiang. Even in exile, he still cared much for his country and people, and expressed these feelings in his poems.", "chunks": ["Qu Yuan was a minister of the State of Chu during the Warring States Period and is recognised as China's first great patriotic poet. In the face of great pressure from the powerful State of Qin, he advocated enriching the country and strengthening its military. However, his reform ideas were opposed by the aristocrats, and he was later deposed (免职) by King Huai and exiled by King Qingxiang. Even in exile, he still cared much for his country and people, and expressed these feelings in his poems."]}, {"id": "u2_s9_p2", "buf": ["When Qu Yuan heard that Qin troops had finally conquered the Chu capital, he finished his final work, which was later included in Odes of Chu, and jumped into the Miluo River. It was the fifth day of the fifth month in the Chinese lunar calendar."], "text": "When Qu Yuan heard that Qin troops had finally conquered the Chu capital, he finished his final work, which was later included in Odes of Chu, and jumped into the Miluo River. It was the fifth day of the fifth month in the Chinese lunar calendar.", "chunks": ["When Qu Yuan heard that Qin troops had finally conquered the Chu capital, he finished his final work, which was later included in Odes of Chu, and jumped into the Miluo River. It was the fifth day of the fifth month in the Chinese lunar calendar."]}, {"id": "u2_s9_p3", "buf": ["realgar wine: a traditional Chinese alcoholic drink made by mixing realgar powder with Chinese wine. In ancient times, it was believed that the drink could drive away evil spirits and get rid of toxins in the body. However, realgar does contain arsenic, which we know today to be poisonous. 雄黄酒，一种加入了雄黄粉的中国传统酒类饮品。在古代，人们认为这种酒能够驱邪并清除体内的毒素。然而，雄黄确实含有砷元素，因此具有毒性。"], "text": "realgar wine: a traditional Chinese alcoholic drink made by mixing realgar powder with Chinese wine. In ancient times, it was believed that the drink could drive away evil spirits and get rid of toxins in the body. However, realgar does contain arsenic, which we know today to be poisonous. 雄黄酒，一种加入了雄黄粉的中国传统酒类饮品。在古代，人们认为这种酒能够驱邪并清除体内的毒素。然而，雄黄确实含有砷元素，因此具有毒性。", "chunks": ["realgar wine: a traditional Chinese alcoholic drink made by mixing realgar powder with Chinese wine. In ancient times, it was believed that the drink could drive away evil spirits and get rid of toxins in the body. However, realgar does contain arsenic, which we know today to be poisonous. 雄黄酒，一种加入了雄黄粉的中国传统酒类饮品。在古代，人们认为这种酒能够驱邪并清除体内的毒素。然而，雄黄确实含有砷元素，因此具有毒性。"]}, {"id": "u2_s9_p4", "buf": ["After his death, the people of the State of Chu gathered along the banks of the river to pay their respects. Fishermen sailed their boats up and down the river looking for his body. To divert (使……转移注意力) fish and shrimps from attacking Qu Yuan's body, people threw zongzi and eggs into the water. An elderly doctor poured a jug of realgar wine into the water, hoping to intoxicate the aquatic creatures so they would do no harm."], "text": "After his death, the people of the State of Chu gathered along the banks of the river to pay their respects. Fishermen sailed their boats up and down the river looking for his body. To divert (使……转移注意力) fish and shrimps from attacking Qu Yuan's body, people threw zongzi and eggs into the water. An elderly doctor poured a jug of realgar wine into the water, hoping to intoxicate the aquatic creatures so they would do no harm.", "chunks": ["After his death, the people of the State of Chu gathered along the banks of the river to pay their respects. Fishermen sailed their boats up and down the river looking for his body. To divert (使……转移注意力) fish and shrimps from attacking Qu Yuan's body, people threw zongzi and eggs into the water. An elderly doctor poured a jug of realgar wine into the water, hoping to intoxicate the aquatic creatures so they would do no harm."]}, {"id": "u2_s9_p5", "buf": ["Today, it has become a popular tradition to eat zongzi and drink realgar wine on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month. It is also a custom to hang mugwort and calamus (菖蒲) on doors or windows to repel insects such as flies, fleas, and moths. These plants are believed to have medicinal properties and to protect people from disease. During the festival, children traditionally wear scented sachets (香囊) threaded with five-coloured silk strings to ward off evil. The scented sachet is worn on the front of one's clothing and often contains cinnabar (朱砂), realgar, and aromatic herbs."], "text": "Today, it has become a popular tradition to eat zongzi and drink realgar wine on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month. It is also a custom to hang mugwort and calamus (菖蒲) on doors or windows to repel insects such as flies, fleas, and moths. These plants are believed to have medicinal properties and to protect people from disease. During the festival, children traditionally wear scented sachets (香囊) threaded with five-coloured silk strings to ward off evil. The scented sachet is worn on the front of one's clothing and often contains cinnabar (朱砂), realgar, and aromatic herbs.", "chunks": ["Today, it has become a popular tradition to eat zongzi and drink realgar wine on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month. It is also a custom to hang mugwort and calamus (菖蒲) on doors or windows to repel insects such as flies, fleas, and moths. These plants are believed to have medicinal properties and to protect people from disease. During the festival, children traditionally wear scented sachets (香囊) threaded with five-coloured silk strings to ward off evil. The scented sachet is worn on the front of one's clothing and often contains cinnabar (朱砂), realgar, and aromatic herbs."]}, {"id": "u2_s9_p6", "buf": ["Dragon boat racing is an indispensable part of the festival. Teams in dragon-shaped boats row to the beat of drums. It has developed into a sport that embodies both Chinese tradition and modern sportsmanship. China's dragon boat racing has grown from a local activity into a grand sports event and has even become an international sporting event."], "text": "Dragon boat racing is an indispensable part of the festival. Teams in dragon-shaped boats row to the beat of drums. It has developed into a sport that embodies both Chinese tradition and modern sportsmanship. China's dragon boat racing has grown from a local activity into a grand sports event and has even become an international sporting event.", "chunks": ["Dragon boat racing is an indispensable part of the festival. Teams in dragon-shaped boats row to the beat of drums. It has developed into a sport that embodies both Chinese tradition and modern sportsmanship. China's dragon boat racing has grown from a local activity into a grand sports event and has even become an international sporting event."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s10", "title": "七夕节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s10_p1", "buf": ["The Double Seventh Festival, celebrated on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, is a traditional festival full of romance."], "text": "The Double Seventh Festival, celebrated on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, is a traditional festival full of romance.", "chunks": ["The Double Seventh Festival, celebrated on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, is a traditional festival full of romance."]}, {"id": "u2_s10_p2", "buf": ["This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated by this celestial river are two bright stars that face each other from afar. One is Vega (织女星), symbolising the Weaver Maid (织女) in China, and the other is Altair (牛郎星), symbolising the Cowhand (牛郎). Their beautiful love story has been passed down from generation to generation."], "text": "This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated by this celestial river are two bright stars that face each other from afar. One is Vega (织女星), symbolising the Weaver Maid (织女) in China, and the other is Altair (牛郎星), symbolising the Cowhand (牛郎). Their beautiful love story has been passed down from generation to generation.", "chunks": ["This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated by this celestial river are two bright stars that face each other from afar. One is Vega (织女星), symbolising the Weaver Maid (织女) in China, and the other is Altair (牛郎星), symbolising the Cowhand (牛郎). Their beautiful love story has been passed down from generation to generation."]}, {"id": "u2_s10_p3", "buf": ["A long time ago, there was an honest and kind-hearted man, the Cowhand, who led a simple life with only one companion—an old ox. One day, a fairy, the Weaver Maid, fell in love with him and secretly came down from heaven to marry him. The Cowhand farmed in the fields while the Weaver Maid wove at home. They lived a happy life and raised a boy and a girl. Unfortunately, the Queen Mother of the West (西王母) discovered their union and ordered her celestial troops to bring the Weaver Maid back to heaven."], "text": "A long time ago, there was an honest and kind-hearted man, the Cowhand, who led a simple life with only one companion—an old ox. One day, a fairy, the Weaver Maid, fell in love with him and secretly came down from heaven to marry him. The Cowhand farmed in the fields while the Weaver Maid wove at home. They lived a happy life and raised a boy and a girl. Unfortunately, the Queen Mother of the West (西王母) discovered their union and ordered her celestial troops to bring the Weaver Maid back to heaven.", "chunks": ["A long time ago, there was an honest and kind-hearted man, the Cowhand, who led a simple life with only one companion—an old ox. One day, a fairy, the Weaver Maid, fell in love with him and secretly came down from heaven to marry him. The Cowhand farmed in the fields while the Weaver Maid wove at home. They lived a happy life and raised a boy and a girl. Unfortunately, the Queen Mother of the West (西王母) discovered their union and ordered her celestial troops to bring the Weaver Maid back to heaven."]}, {"id": "u2_s10_p4", "buf": ["With the help of his ox, the Cowhand flew up to heaven with his son and daughter. Just as he was about to reach his wife, the Queen Mother of the West took off one of her hairpins (发簪) and drew a line across the sky. Immediately, a celestial river appeared. The Cowhand and the Weaver Maid were separated by this river forever, and they could only shed their tears. Their love touched the hearts of magpies (喜鹊), who flew together to form a bridge to enable them to meet. Eventually, the Queen Mother of the West was moved and allowed them to reunite once a year on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month. Hence, their meeting date is called qixi (the evening of the seventh day of the seventh lunar month)."], "text": "With the help of his ox, the Cowhand flew up to heaven with his son and daughter. Just as he was about to reach his wife, the Queen Mother of the West took off one of her hairpins (发簪) and drew a line across the sky. Immediately, a celestial river appeared. The Cowhand and the Weaver Maid were separated by this river forever, and they could only shed their tears. Their love touched the hearts of magpies (喜鹊), who flew together to form a bridge to enable them to meet. Eventually, the Queen Mother of the West was moved and allowed them to reunite once a year on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month. Hence, their meeting date is called qixi (the evening of the seventh day of the seventh lunar month).", "chunks": ["With the help of his ox, the Cowhand flew up to heaven with his son and daughter. Just as he was about to reach his wife, the Queen Mother of the West took off one of her hairpins (发簪) and drew a line across the sky. Immediately, a celestial river appeared. The Cowhand and the Weaver Maid were separated by this river forever, and they could only shed their tears. Their love touched the hearts of magpies (喜鹊), who flew together to form a bridge to enable them to meet. Eventually, the Queen Mother of the West was moved and allowed them to reunite once a year on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month. Hence, their meeting date is called qixi (the evening of the seventh day of the seventh lunar month)."]}, {"id": "u2_s10_p5", "buf": ["Today, the Double Seventh Festival is regarded as Chinese Valentine's Day. On this day, more and more people go on dates and exchange gifts to express their affection. Long queues form at the gate of the local Civil Affairs Bureau (民政局), where couples rush to register their marriages. The festival conveys a symbolic message: choose your own love and remain faithful."], "text": "Today, the Double Seventh Festival is regarded as Chinese Valentine's Day. On this day, more and more people go on dates and exchange gifts to express their affection. Long queues form at the gate of the local Civil Affairs Bureau (民政局), where couples rush to register their marriages. The festival conveys a symbolic message: choose your own love and remain faithful.", "chunks": ["Today, the Double Seventh Festival is regarded as Chinese Valentine's Day. On this day, more and more people go on dates and exchange gifts to express their affection. Long queues form at the gate of the local Civil Affairs Bureau (民政局), where couples rush to register their marriages. The festival conveys a symbolic message: choose your own love and remain faithful."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s11", "title": "中秋节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s11_p1", "buf": ["The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month. The story below is one of the many legends about the festival."], "text": "The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month. The story below is one of the many legends about the festival.", "chunks": ["The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month. The story below is one of the many legends about the festival."]}, {"id": "u2_s11_p2", "buf": ["In the distant past, there were 10 suns in the sky, whose scorching heat burnt all the crops, dried up all the lakes, and put people at death's door. A hero named Yi (羿) was deeply troubled by people's suffering. He climbed Mount Kunlun, drew his extraordinary bow, and shot down nine suns, leaving only one to benefit people. After this, he was respected and loved by the people."], "text": "In the distant past, there were 10 suns in the sky, whose scorching heat burnt all the crops, dried up all the lakes, and put people at death's door. A hero named Yi (羿) was deeply troubled by people's suffering. He climbed Mount Kunlun, drew his extraordinary bow, and shot down nine suns, leaving only one to benefit people. After this, he was respected and loved by the people.", "chunks": ["In the distant past, there were 10 suns in the sky, whose scorching heat burnt all the crops, dried up all the lakes, and put people at death's door. A hero named Yi (羿) was deeply troubled by people's suffering. He climbed Mount Kunlun, drew his extraordinary bow, and shot down nine suns, leaving only one to benefit people. After this, he was respected and loved by the people."]}, {"id": "u2_s11_p3", "buf": ["To reward his deeds, the Queen Mother of the West granted him an elixir of immortality. One day, while Yi was away hunting, his disciple Pang Meng (逢蒙) tried to seize the elixir. In desperation, Yi's wife, the gentle Chang'e, swallowed it herself. At once, she floated upwards and ascended to the moon, where she began her solitary life in the cold, quiet Moon Palace."], "text": "To reward his deeds, the Queen Mother of the West granted him an elixir of immortality. One day, while Yi was away hunting, his disciple Pang Meng (逢蒙) tried to seize the elixir. In desperation, Yi's wife, the gentle Chang'e, swallowed it herself. At once, she floated upwards and ascended to the moon, where she began her solitary life in the cold, quiet Moon Palace.", "chunks": ["To reward his deeds, the Queen Mother of the West granted him an elixir of immortality. One day, while Yi was away hunting, his disciple Pang Meng (逢蒙) tried to seize the elixir. In desperation, Yi's wife, the gentle Chang'e, swallowed it herself. At once, she floated upwards and ascended to the moon, where she began her solitary life in the cold, quiet Moon Palace."]}, {"id": "u2_s11_p4", "buf": ["Overcome with grief, Yi looked up into the night sky and called out the name of his beloved wife. He found that the moon was exceptionally clear and bright, and on it was a shimmering shadow that looked exactly like his wife. But he could not reach her on the moon. Moved by his deep devotion, people began placing offerings under the moon to pray for happiness and reunion, and thus the Mid-Autumn Festival gradually became a folk tradition."], "text": "Overcome with grief, Yi looked up into the night sky and called out the name of his beloved wife. He found that the moon was exceptionally clear and bright, and on it was a shimmering shadow that looked exactly like his wife. But he could not reach her on the moon. Moved by his deep devotion, people began placing offerings under the moon to pray for happiness and reunion, and thus the Mid-Autumn Festival gradually became a folk tradition.", "chunks": ["Overcome with grief, Yi looked up into the night sky and called out the name of his beloved wife. He found that the moon was exceptionally clear and bright, and on it was a shimmering shadow that looked exactly like his wife. But he could not reach her on the moon. Moved by his deep devotion, people began placing offerings under the moon to pray for happiness and reunion, and thus the Mid-Autumn Festival gradually became a folk tradition."]}, {"id": "u2_s11_p5", "buf": ["Since the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month is in the season when crops and fruits ripen and the weather is pleasant, families and friends gather outdoors on this day, with food on tables, to appreciate the full and bright moon. People away from home often recite Li Bai's famous lines, \"Looking up, I find the moon bright; Bowing, in homesickness I'm drowned. (举头望明月，低头思故乡。)\" Others who long for reunion and enduring affection prefer Su Shi's lines, \"So let us wish that man may live long as he can! Though miles apart, we'll share the beauty she (the moon) displays. (但愿人长久，千里共婵娟。)\" These verses express feelings of longing, homesickness, and the hope of remaining connected with loved ones despite their distance."], "text": "Since the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month is in the season when crops and fruits ripen and the weather is pleasant, families and friends gather outdoors on this day, with food on tables, to appreciate the full and bright moon. People away from home often recite Li Bai's famous lines, \"Looking up, I find the moon bright; Bowing, in homesickness I'm drowned. (举头望明月，低头思故乡。)\" Others who long for reunion and enduring affection prefer Su Shi's lines, \"So let us wish that man may live long as he can! Though miles apart, we'll share the beauty she (the moon) displays. (但愿人长久，千里共婵娟。)\" These verses express feelings of longing, homesickness, and the hope of remaining connected with loved ones despite their distance.", "chunks": ["Since the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month is in the season when crops and fruits ripen and the weather is pleasant, families and friends gather outdoors on this day, with food on tables, to appreciate the full and bright moon. People away from home often recite Li Bai's famous lines, \"Looking up, I find the moon bright; Bowing, in homesickness I'm drowned. (举头望明月，低头思故乡。)\" Others who long for reunion and enduring affection prefer Su Shi's lines, \"So let us wish that man may live long as he can! Though miles apart, we'll share the beauty she (the moon) displays. (但愿人长久，千里共婵娟。)\" These verses express feelings of longing, homesickness, and the hope of remaining connected with loved ones despite their distance."]}, {"id": "u2_s11_p6", "buf": ["Most commonly enjoyed during the Mid-Autumn Festival, mooncakes are traditional Chinese pastries that symbolise reunion and harmony. Since the festival is also the time when sweet-scented osmanthus flowers (桂花) are in full bloom, enjoying osmanthus-flavoured cakes and osmanthus wine while appreciating the full moon is a popular part of the celebration."], "text": "Most commonly enjoyed during the Mid-Autumn Festival, mooncakes are traditional Chinese pastries that symbolise reunion and harmony. Since the festival is also the time when sweet-scented osmanthus flowers (桂花) are in full bloom, enjoying osmanthus-flavoured cakes and osmanthus wine while appreciating the full moon is a popular part of the celebration.", "chunks": ["Most commonly enjoyed during the Mid-Autumn Festival, mooncakes are traditional Chinese pastries that symbolise reunion and harmony. Since the festival is also the time when sweet-scented osmanthus flowers (桂花) are in full bloom, enjoying osmanthus-flavoured cakes and osmanthus wine while appreciating the full moon is a popular part of the celebration."]}]}, {"id": "u2_s12", "title": "重阳节", "paras": [{"id": "u2_s12_p1", "buf": ["The Double Ninth Festival, or Chongyang Festival, is celebrated on the ninth day of the ninth lunar month. The festival dates back to the Warring States Period. According to The Book of Changes, the number nine represents yang. Thus, the occurrence of \"nine\" in both the month and the day led to the name \"Chongyang (重阳)\", with chong meaning \"double\" in Chinese. Chinese ancestors regarded this \"double yang\" day as highly auspicious, and the festival has been celebrated since ancient times."], "text": "The Double Ninth Festival, or Chongyang Festival, is celebrated on the ninth day of the ninth lunar month. The festival dates back to the Warring States Period. According to The Book of Changes, the number nine represents yang. Thus, the occurrence of \"nine\" in both the month and the day led to the name \"Chongyang (重阳)\", with chong meaning \"double\" in Chinese. Chinese ancestors regarded this \"double yang\" day as highly auspicious, and the festival has been celebrated since ancient times.", "chunks": ["The Double Ninth Festival, or Chongyang Festival, is celebrated on the ninth day of the ninth lunar month. The festival dates back to the Warring States Period. According to The Book of Changes, the number nine represents yang. Thus, the occurrence of \"nine\" in both the month and the day led to the name \"Chongyang (重阳)\", with chong meaning \"double\" in Chinese. Chinese ancestors regarded this \"double yang\" day as highly auspicious, and the festival has been celebrated since ancient times."]}, {"id": "u2_s12_p2", "buf": ["The celebration of the festival is vibrant and typically includes a variety of activities such as outings, mountain climbing, appreciating chrysanthemums (菊花), wearing dogwood (茱萸), eating Chongyang cake, and drinking chrysanthemum wine."], "text": "The celebration of the festival is vibrant and typically includes a variety of activities such as outings, mountain climbing, appreciating chrysanthemums (菊花), wearing dogwood (茱萸), eating Chongyang cake, and drinking chrysanthemum wine.", "chunks": ["The celebration of the festival is vibrant and typically includes a variety of activities such as outings, mountain climbing, appreciating chrysanthemums (菊花), wearing dogwood (茱萸), eating Chongyang cake, and drinking chrysanthemum wine."]}, {"id": "u2_s12_p3", "buf": ["In 2013, the Chinese government designated the ninth day of the ninth lunar month each year as the country's Seniors' Day (老年节), marking the official recognition of the Double Ninth Festival at the national level. Government offices, organisations, and communities often organise autumn trips for senior citizens, and younger generations often accompany older family members on outings or present them with gifts as tokens of respect."], "text": "In 2013, the Chinese government designated the ninth day of the ninth lunar month each year as the country's Seniors' Day (老年节), marking the official recognition of the Double Ninth Festival at the national level. Government offices, organisations, and communities often organise autumn trips for senior citizens, and younger generations often accompany older family members on outings or present them with gifts as tokens of respect.", "chunks": ["In 2013, the Chinese government designated the ninth day of the ninth lunar month each year as the country's Seniors' Day (老年节), marking the official recognition of the Double Ninth Festival at the national level. Government offices, organisations, and communities often organise autumn trips for senior citizens, and younger generations often accompany older family members on outings or present them with gifts as tokens of respect."]}]}]}, {"id": "u3", "title": "少数民族节日", "sections": [{"id": "u3_s0", "title": "少数民族节日", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s0_p1", "buf": ["China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing Festival (泼水节) of the Dai people, the Nadam Fair (那达慕) of the Mongolian people, the Torch Festival (火把节) of the Yi people, the Danu Festival (达努节) of the Yao people, the Third Month Fair (三月街节) of the Bai people, the Gexu Singing Festival (歌圩节) of the Zhuang people, the Tibetan New Year (藏历新年) of the Tibetan people, and the Flower-Jumping Festival (跳花节) of the Miao people."], "text": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing Festival (泼水节) of the Dai people, the Nadam Fair (那达慕) of the Mongolian people, the Torch Festival (火把节) of the Yi people, the Danu Festival (达努节) of the Yao people, the Third Month Fair (三月街节) of the Bai people, the Gexu Singing Festival (歌圩节) of the Zhuang people, the Tibetan New Year (藏历新年) of the Tibetan people, and the Flower-Jumping Festival (跳花节) of the Miao people.", "chunks": ["China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing Festival (泼水节) of the Dai people, the Nadam Fair (那达慕) of the Mongolian people, the Torch Festival (火把节) of the Yi people, the Danu Festival (达努节) of the Yao people, the Third Month Fair (三月街节) of the Bai people, the Gexu Singing Festival (歌圩节) of the Zhuang people, the Tibetan New Year (藏历新年) of the Tibetan people, and the Flower-Jumping Festival (跳花节) of the Miao people."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s1", "title": "泼水节", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s1_p1", "buf": ["The Water-Splashing Festival, celebrated in southwestern China's Yunnan Province, is a carnival of entertainment and culture. It is the most important traditional festival for the Dai people and some other ethnic groups, marking their New Year. The festival usually lasts for about three days, with water splashing as the central activity, as the name suggests."], "text": "The Water-Splashing Festival, celebrated in southwestern China's Yunnan Province, is a carnival of entertainment and culture. It is the most important traditional festival for the Dai people and some other ethnic groups, marking their New Year. The festival usually lasts for about three days, with water splashing as the central activity, as the name suggests.", "chunks": ["The Water-Splashing Festival, celebrated in southwestern China's Yunnan Province, is a carnival of entertainment and culture. It is the most important traditional festival for the Dai people and some other ethnic groups, marking their New Year. The festival usually lasts for about three days, with water splashing as the central activity, as the name suggests."]}, {"id": "u3_s1_p2", "buf": ["The first day of the festival is the eve. On this day, the Dai people engage in activities such as rocket launching and dragon boat racing. The rockets are bamboo pipes filled with gunpowder, which are fired into the sky, leaving trails of smoke behind. The second day is the \"Empty Day (空日)\", which belongs to neither the old year nor the new year. The third day is New Year's Day. People put on their best clothes and go to temples to bathe Buddha statues with clean water and listen to Buddhist scriptures. After the ceremony, water-splashing festivities begin, during which people drench one another to bestow blessings of good luck, prosperity, and good health for the new year."], "text": "The first day of the festival is the eve. On this day, the Dai people engage in activities such as rocket launching and dragon boat racing. The rockets are bamboo pipes filled with gunpowder, which are fired into the sky, leaving trails of smoke behind. The second day is the \"Empty Day (空日)\", which belongs to neither the old year nor the new year. The third day is New Year's Day. People put on their best clothes and go to temples to bathe Buddha statues with clean water and listen to Buddhist scriptures. After the ceremony, water-splashing festivities begin, during which people drench one another to bestow blessings of good luck, prosperity, and good health for the new year.", "chunks": ["The first day of the festival is the eve. On this day, the Dai people engage in activities such as rocket launching and dragon boat racing. The rockets are bamboo pipes filled with gunpowder, which are fired into the sky, leaving trails of smoke behind. The second day is the \"Empty Day (空日)\", which belongs to neither the old year nor the new year. The third day is New Year's Day. People put on their best clothes and go to temples to bathe Buddha statues with clean water and listen to Buddhist scriptures. After the ceremony, water-splashing festivities begin, during which people drench one another to bestow blessings of good luck, prosperity, and good health for the new year."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s2", "title": "那达慕", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s2_p1", "buf": ["The Nadam Fair is a traditional festival observed by the Mongolian people. It takes place in July and August when the pastures (牧场) are rich in water, the grass is lush (茂盛的), the herds are thriving, and the weather is fine. The festival is a large gathering for entertainment and a celebration of the year's harvest."], "text": "The Nadam Fair is a traditional festival observed by the Mongolian people. It takes place in July and August when the pastures (牧场) are rich in water, the grass is lush (茂盛的), the herds are thriving, and the weather is fine. The festival is a large gathering for entertainment and a celebration of the year's harvest.", "chunks": ["The Nadam Fair is a traditional festival observed by the Mongolian people. It takes place in July and August when the pastures (牧场) are rich in water, the grass is lush (茂盛的), the herds are thriving, and the weather is fine. The festival is a large gathering for entertainment and a celebration of the year's harvest."]}, {"id": "u3_s2_p2", "buf": ["The main events of the Nadam Fair include horse racing, wrestling, archery, and theatrical performances. To the Mongolian people, the Nadam Fair is ancient, sacred, grand, and full of joy. Nowadays, it has become a large-scale event featuring ceremonial rites, celebrations, athletic competitions, entertainment, and trade. Agricultural and animal products, handicrafts, and other local specialities from different regions are exchanged at the fair."], "text": "The main events of the Nadam Fair include horse racing, wrestling, archery, and theatrical performances. To the Mongolian people, the Nadam Fair is ancient, sacred, grand, and full of joy. Nowadays, it has become a large-scale event featuring ceremonial rites, celebrations, athletic competitions, entertainment, and trade. Agricultural and animal products, handicrafts, and other local specialities from different regions are exchanged at the fair.", "chunks": ["The main events of the Nadam Fair include horse racing, wrestling, archery, and theatrical performances. To the Mongolian people, the Nadam Fair is ancient, sacred, grand, and full of joy. Nowadays, it has become a large-scale event featuring ceremonial rites, celebrations, athletic competitions, entertainment, and trade. Agricultural and animal products, handicrafts, and other local specialities from different regions are exchanged at the fair."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s3", "title": "火把节", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s3_p1", "buf": ["The Torch Festival is a traditional festival of the Yi, Naxi, Lahu (拉祜族) ethnic groups, and some other ethnic minority groups inhabiting southwestern China. It falls on or around the twenty-fourth of the sixth lunar month. On the day of the festival, people build big, pagoda-shaped torches that are made of pinewood and wrapped in layers of dry firewood and pine branches. As night falls, a large torch is lit in the village, while each household lights smaller ones. With their flaming torches held high, the villagers walk around their houses and fields. People then meet at the bonfire to sing and dance, pray for a good harvest in the coming year, and strengthen social bonds."], "text": "The Torch Festival is a traditional festival of the Yi, Naxi, Lahu (拉祜族) ethnic groups, and some other ethnic minority groups inhabiting southwestern China. It falls on or around the twenty-fourth of the sixth lunar month. On the day of the festival, people build big, pagoda-shaped torches that are made of pinewood and wrapped in layers of dry firewood and pine branches. As night falls, a large torch is lit in the village, while each household lights smaller ones. With their flaming torches held high, the villagers walk around their houses and fields. People then meet at the bonfire to sing and dance, pray for a good harvest in the coming year, and strengthen social bonds.", "chunks": ["The Torch Festival is a traditional festival of the Yi, Naxi, Lahu (拉祜族) ethnic groups, and some other ethnic minority groups inhabiting southwestern China. It falls on or around the twenty-fourth of the sixth lunar month. On the day of the festival, people build big, pagoda-shaped torches that are made of pinewood and wrapped in layers of dry firewood and pine branches. As night falls, a large torch is lit in the village, while each household lights smaller ones. With their flaming torches held high, the villagers walk around their houses and fields. People then meet at the bonfire to sing and dance, pray for a good harvest in the coming year, and strengthen social bonds."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s4", "title": "达努节", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s4_p1", "buf": ["Danu means \"never forget\" in the Yao language. The Danu Festival commemorates the ancestor of the Yao ethnic group and also serves as the Yao people's New Year celebration. It begins on the twenty-ninth of the fifth lunar month. On the day of the festival, everyone dresses smartly, enjoys dishes of chicken and mutton, and drinks long-preserved rice wine. The festival also features entertainments like performing the bronze drum dance and antiphonal singing (对歌)."], "text": "Danu means \"never forget\" in the Yao language. The Danu Festival commemorates the ancestor of the Yao ethnic group and also serves as the Yao people's New Year celebration. It begins on the twenty-ninth of the fifth lunar month. On the day of the festival, everyone dresses smartly, enjoys dishes of chicken and mutton, and drinks long-preserved rice wine. The festival also features entertainments like performing the bronze drum dance and antiphonal singing (对歌).", "chunks": ["Danu means \"never forget\" in the Yao language. The Danu Festival commemorates the ancestor of the Yao ethnic group and also serves as the Yao people's New Year celebration. It begins on the twenty-ninth of the fifth lunar month. On the day of the festival, everyone dresses smartly, enjoys dishes of chicken and mutton, and drinks long-preserved rice wine. The festival also features entertainments like performing the bronze drum dance and antiphonal singing (对歌)."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s5", "title": "三月街", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s5_p1", "buf": ["The Third Month Fair is a festival celebrated by the Bai people. It lasts from the fifteenth to the twenty-first of the third lunar month. Activities during the festival include horse racing, archery, singing, dancing, and a commodity fair with a distinctive ethnic flavour. The Third Month Fair is also an occasion for romance. Young men and women meet and get to know each other through singing."], "text": "The Third Month Fair is a festival celebrated by the Bai people. It lasts from the fifteenth to the twenty-first of the third lunar month. Activities during the festival include horse racing, archery, singing, dancing, and a commodity fair with a distinctive ethnic flavour. The Third Month Fair is also an occasion for romance. Young men and women meet and get to know each other through singing.", "chunks": ["The Third Month Fair is a festival celebrated by the Bai people. It lasts from the fifteenth to the twenty-first of the third lunar month. Activities during the festival include horse racing, archery, singing, dancing, and a commodity fair with a distinctive ethnic flavour. The Third Month Fair is also an occasion for romance. Young men and women meet and get to know each other through singing."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s6", "title": "歌圩节", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s6_p1", "buf": ["The Gexu Singing Festival is a traditional festival for the Zhuang people. It is held on the third day of the third lunar month. At the festival, young people, dressed in their best attire, gather in the fields, atop hills, among bamboo groves, or on grass-covered hillsides to engage in antiphonal singing in groups with those of the opposite sex. They sing from morning to night, their songs resonating far and wide."], "text": "The Gexu Singing Festival is a traditional festival for the Zhuang people. It is held on the third day of the third lunar month. At the festival, young people, dressed in their best attire, gather in the fields, atop hills, among bamboo groves, or on grass-covered hillsides to engage in antiphonal singing in groups with those of the opposite sex. They sing from morning to night, their songs resonating far and wide.", "chunks": ["The Gexu Singing Festival is a traditional festival for the Zhuang people. It is held on the third day of the third lunar month. At the festival, young people, dressed in their best attire, gather in the fields, atop hills, among bamboo groves, or on grass-covered hillsides to engage in antiphonal singing in groups with those of the opposite sex. They sing from morning to night, their songs resonating far and wide."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s7", "title": "藏历新年", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s7_p1", "buf": ["The Tibetan New Year is the most important festival for Tibetan people. It lasts for fifteen days, beginning on the first day of the first month in the Tibetan calendar. On the eve of the Tibetan New Year, Tibetans make thorough preparations for the celebration, such as cleaning and decorating their homes. In the evening, an auspicious dinner is offered. On the first day of the Tibetan New Year, they stay at home to celebrate with family members. On the second day, they visit relatives and friends, extending greetings to each other with the traditional greeting \"tashi delek (扎西德勒)\", meaning \"good luck\". On the third day, each Tibetan family puts up a prayer flag that will fly on their rooftop for the whole year. Mass singing and dancing, as well as traditional Tibetan operas, are performed in towns and villages during this period."], "text": "The Tibetan New Year is the most important festival for Tibetan people. It lasts for fifteen days, beginning on the first day of the first month in the Tibetan calendar. On the eve of the Tibetan New Year, Tibetans make thorough preparations for the celebration, such as cleaning and decorating their homes. In the evening, an auspicious dinner is offered. On the first day of the Tibetan New Year, they stay at home to celebrate with family members. On the second day, they visit relatives and friends, extending greetings to each other with the traditional greeting \"tashi delek (扎西德勒)\", meaning \"good luck\". On the third day, each Tibetan family puts up a prayer flag that will fly on their rooftop for the whole year. Mass singing and dancing, as well as traditional Tibetan operas, are performed in towns and villages during this period.", "chunks": ["The Tibetan New Year is the most important festival for Tibetan people. It lasts for fifteen days, beginning on the first day of the first month in the Tibetan calendar. On the eve of the Tibetan New Year, Tibetans make thorough preparations for the celebration, such as cleaning and decorating their homes. In the evening, an auspicious dinner is offered. On the first day of the Tibetan New Year, they stay at home to celebrate with family members. On the second day, they visit relatives and friends, extending greetings to each other with the traditional greeting \"tashi delek (扎西德勒)\", meaning \"good luck\". On the third day, each Tibetan family puts up a prayer flag that will fly on their rooftop for the whole year. Mass singing and dancing, as well as traditional Tibetan operas, are performed in towns and villages during this period."]}]}, {"id": "u3_s8", "title": "跳花节", "paras": [{"id": "u3_s8_p1", "buf": ["The Flower-Jumping Festival is celebrated by the Miao people in Guizhou Province and other Miao-inhabited areas between the seventh and ninth days of the first lunar month. During this annual festival, thousands of Miao people gather on open hillsides and hold various festive activities, including singing, reed-pipe dance (芦笙舞), bull fighting, and a flower-pole-climbing contest, in which the champion is awarded a pig's head and fine wine."], "text": "The Flower-Jumping Festival is celebrated by the Miao people in Guizhou Province and other Miao-inhabited areas between the seventh and ninth days of the first lunar month. During this annual festival, thousands of Miao people gather on open hillsides and hold various festive activities, including singing, reed-pipe dance (芦笙舞), bull fighting, and a flower-pole-climbing contest, in which the champion is awarded a pig's head and fine wine.", "chunks": ["The Flower-Jumping Festival is celebrated by the Miao people in Guizhou Province and other Miao-inhabited areas between the seventh and ninth days of the first lunar month. During this annual festival, thousands of Miao people gather on open hillsides and hold various festive activities, including singing, reed-pipe dance (芦笙舞), bull fighting, and a flower-pole-climbing contest, in which the champion is awarded a pig's head and fine wine."]}]}]}], "vocabA": [{"term": "Spring Festival couplets", "zh": "春联", "q": "From then on, on every New Year's Eve, families paste Spring Festival couplets (春联) on their doors, set off firecrackers, keep the lights on, and stay up late to keep Nian away. The next day, which is", "tr": "从此，每逢除夕，家家户户都要在门上贴春联、放鞭炮、通宵亮灯、守岁熬夜，以驱赶年兽。第二天，也就是农历新年的第一天，人们一早就走亲访友，相互问候道贺。随着时间推移，这些习俗流传甚广，世代相传。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "ingot", "zh": "元宝", "q": "Houses are brightly lit, and a sumptuous (丰盛的) family dinner is served on Spring Festival Eve. Dishes such as chicken, fish, and tofu are traditionally included, as their pronunciations are similar to", "tr": "除夕之夜，家家灯火通明，摆下丰盛的年夜饭。菜肴中通常少不了鸡、鱼和豆腐，因为它们的读音分别与\"吉\"\"余\"\"福\"相近，寓意吉祥、富足和福气。在南方，人们吃年糕，象征\"年年高升\"的美好愿望；在北方，人们更爱吃饺子，认为它寓意辞旧迎新。这一说法源于\"饺子\"与\"更岁交子\"谐音，\"更岁交子\"即旧年与新年交替之意。饺子的形状酷似中国古代的元宝，因此吃饺子也寄托着招财进宝、富足兴旺的愿望。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "change of year", "zh": "更岁交子", "q": "Houses are brightly lit, and a sumptuous (丰盛的) family dinner is served on Spring Festival Eve. Dishes such as chicken, fish, and tofu are traditionally included, as their pronunciations are similar to", "tr": "除夕之夜，家家灯火通明，摆下丰盛的年夜饭。菜肴中通常少不了鸡、鱼和豆腐，因为它们的读音分别与\"吉\"\"余\"\"福\"相近，寓意吉祥、富足和福气。在南方，人们吃年糕，象征\"年年高升\"的美好愿望；在北方，人们更爱吃饺子，认为它寓意辞旧迎新。这一说法源于\"饺子\"与\"更岁交子\"谐音，\"更岁交子\"即旧年与新年交替之意。饺子的形状酷似中国古代的元宝，因此吃饺子也寄托着招财进宝、富足兴旺的愿望。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "sesame", "zh": "芝麻", "q": "Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also kno", "tr": "元宵节的另一项重要习俗是吃汤圆。这种食物在北方多称\"元宵\"，在南方多称\"汤圆\"。元宵或汤圆外形圆润，与团圆相聚相关联，人们吃它，寓意来年阖家团圆、和睦幸福。元宵或汤圆是用糯米粉做成的小圆子，常见的馅料有芝麻、豆沙、枣泥、核桃仁或干果，可以煮、炸或蒸。因此，元宵节又被许多人称为\"元宵佳节\"。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "bean paste", "zh": "豆沙", "q": "Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also kno", "tr": "元宵节的另一项重要习俗是吃汤圆。这种食物在北方多称\"元宵\"，在南方多称\"汤圆\"。元宵或汤圆外形圆润，与团圆相聚相关联，人们吃它，寓意来年阖家团圆、和睦幸福。元宵或汤圆是用糯米粉做成的小圆子，常见的馅料有芝麻、豆沙、枣泥、核桃仁或干果，可以煮、炸或蒸。因此，元宵节又被许多人称为\"元宵佳节\"。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "date paste", "zh": "枣泥", "q": "Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also kno", "tr": "元宵节的另一项重要习俗是吃汤圆。这种食物在北方多称\"元宵\"，在南方多称\"汤圆\"。元宵或汤圆外形圆润，与团圆相聚相关联，人们吃它，寓意来年阖家团圆、和睦幸福。元宵或汤圆是用糯米粉做成的小圆子，常见的馅料有芝麻、豆沙、枣泥、核桃仁或干果，可以煮、炸或蒸。因此，元宵节又被许多人称为\"元宵佳节\"。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "walnuts", "zh": "核桃仁", "q": "Another important part of the Lantern Festival is eating glutinous rice balls, which are more frequently called yuanxiao in the north and tangyuan in the south. Hence, the Lantern Festival is also kno", "tr": "元宵节的另一项重要习俗是吃汤圆。这种食物在北方多称\"元宵\"，在南方多称\"汤圆\"。元宵或汤圆外形圆润，与团圆相聚相关联，人们吃它，寓意来年阖家团圆、和睦幸福。元宵或汤圆是用糯米粉做成的小圆子，常见的馅料有芝麻、豆沙、枣泥、核桃仁或干果，可以煮、炸或蒸。因此，元宵节又被许多人称为\"元宵佳节\"。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "spring", "zh": "春", "q": "③ How does your family celebrate the Spring Festival?", "tr": "③ 你家是怎样过春节的？", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "wealth", "zh": "财", "q": "Decorations are an important feature of the Spring Festival celebrations. Traditional Chinese paper cuttings are pasted on windows. The paper used is usually red, and the designs may include birds, fl", "tr": "装饰是春节庆祝活动的重要特色。人们把传统的中国剪纸贴在窗上，所用纸张通常为红色，图案包括花鸟、鱼和风景等。家家户户还在大门上贴对联，对联用书法写成，象征着万象更新、春回大地。除了贴对联，人们还常张贴写有\"春\"\"财\"\"福\"等字的书法作品。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Pure Brightness solar term", "zh": "清明节气", "q": "The Qingming Festival, also known as Tomb-Sweeping Day, is a day for mourning (哀悼) the dead and honouring ancestors. It usually falls between 4th April and 6th April, the first day of the Pure Brightn", "tr": "清明节又称\"扫墓日\"，是哀悼逝者、祭祀祖先的日子。它通常在4月4日至6日之间，也就是二十四节气中\"清明\"节气的第一天，而清明正是中国传统历法二十四节气之一。清明前后，气温回升、降雨增多，标志着春耕和播种的开始。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "spring ploughing", "zh": "春耕", "q": "The Qingming Festival, also known as Tomb-Sweeping Day, is a day for mourning (哀悼) the dead and honouring ancestors. It usually falls between 4th April and 6th April, the first day of the Pure Brightn", "tr": "清明节又称\"扫墓日\"，是哀悼逝者、祭祀祖先的日子。它通常在4月4日至6日之间，也就是二十四节气中\"清明\"节气的第一天，而清明正是中国传统历法二十四节气之一。清明前后，气温回升、降雨增多，标志着春耕和播种的开始。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Cold Food Day", "zh": "寒食节", "q": "The Hanshi Festival, or Cold Food Day (寒食节), is observed one or two days before the Qingming Festival. On this day, people traditionally refrain from using fire and eat only cold food. The custom comm", "tr": "寒食节在清明节的前一两天。这一天，按传统人们不生火做饭，只吃冷食。这一习俗是为了纪念春秋时期辅佐公子重耳的忠臣介子推。相传，介子推曾割下自己腿上的肉，给流亡中的主君充饥。后来重耳成为国君，曾放火烧山，想逼介子推回朝；介子推宁可与母亲一起隐居，最终死于大火。为悼念他的牺牲，这位国君下令在这一天禁止生火，寒食节便由此而来。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "joss paper", "zh": "纸钱", "q": "One of the major customs of the Qingming Festival is tomb sweeping. On this day, people commemorate and honour the dead by cleaning graves and, in traditional practice, by burning incense (祭祀时用的香) and", "tr": "清明节的主要习俗之一是扫墓。这一天，人们通过清理坟茔来缅怀、祭祀逝者；传统做法还包括焚香、烧纸钱，以及供奉食物、酒水等。随着时间推移，这一习俗逐渐被更环保的方式取代。一些人以献花来追思亲人，而那些无法前往墓地的人则通过网络祭扫来表达悼念。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "mugwort", "zh": "艾草", "q": "In southern China, people typically eat qingtuan, which are made with glutinous rice flour mixed with the juice of Chinese mugwort (艾草) or barley grass (大麦草). In northern China, people eat cold foods ", "tr": "在南方，人们通常吃青团，它是用糯米粉掺入艾草或大麦草的汁液制成的。在北方，人们则吃冷粥、冷饼等冷食。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "barley grass", "zh": "大麦草", "q": "In southern China, people typically eat qingtuan, which are made with glutinous rice flour mixed with the juice of Chinese mugwort (艾草) or barley grass (大麦草). In northern China, people eat cold foods ", "tr": "在南方，人们通常吃青团，它是用糯米粉掺入艾草或大麦草的汁液制成的。在北方，人们则吃冷粥、冷饼等冷食。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "realgar wine", "zh": "雄黄酒", "q": "realgar wine: a traditional Chinese alcoholic drink made by mixing realgar powder with Chinese wine. In ancient times, it was believed that the drink could drive away evil spirits and get rid of toxin", "tr": "雄黄酒是一种加入了雄黄粉的中国传统酒类饮品。在古代，人们认为这种酒能够驱邪，并清除体内的毒素。然而，雄黄确实含有砷元素，因此具有毒性。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "calamus", "zh": "菖蒲", "q": "Today, it has become a popular tradition to eat zongzi and drink realgar wine on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month. It is also a custom to hang mugwort and calamus (菖蒲) on doors or windows to rep", "tr": "如今，在农历五月初五吃粽子、喝雄黄酒已成为一项广为流传的传统。人们还有在门上或窗上悬挂艾草和菖蒲的习俗，用以驱赶苍蝇、跳蚤、飞蛾等昆虫。人们相信这些植物具有药用价值，能够防病祛疾。节日期间，孩子们按传统会佩戴用五彩丝线串起的香囊，以辟邪驱恶。香囊佩戴在衣服前襟，里面通常装有朱砂、雄黄和芳香草药。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "scented sachets", "zh": "香囊", "q": "Today, it has become a popular tradition to eat zongzi and drink realgar wine on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month. It is also a custom to hang mugwort and calamus (菖蒲) on doors or windows to rep", "tr": "如今，在农历五月初五吃粽子、喝雄黄酒已成为一项广为流传的传统。人们还有在门上或窗上悬挂艾草和菖蒲的习俗，用以驱赶苍蝇、跳蚤、飞蛾等昆虫。人们相信这些植物具有药用价值，能够防病祛疾。节日期间，孩子们按传统会佩戴用五彩丝线串起的香囊，以辟邪驱恶。香囊佩戴在衣服前襟，里面通常装有朱砂、雄黄和芳香草药。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "cinnabar", "zh": "朱砂", "q": "Today, it has become a popular tradition to eat zongzi and drink realgar wine on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month. It is also a custom to hang mugwort and calamus (菖蒲) on doors or windows to rep", "tr": "如今，在农历五月初五吃粽子、喝雄黄酒已成为一项广为流传的传统。人们还有在门上或窗上悬挂艾草和菖蒲的习俗，用以驱赶苍蝇、跳蚤、飞蛾等昆虫。人们相信这些植物具有药用价值，能够防病祛疾。节日期间，孩子们按传统会佩戴用五彩丝线串起的香囊，以辟邪驱恶。香囊佩戴在衣服前襟，里面通常装有朱砂、雄黄和芳香草药。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Dragon Boat Race", "zh": "龙舟赛", "q": "", "tr": "", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Vega", "zh": "织女星", "q": "This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated ", "tr": "七夕节时值盛夏，天气炎热，草木葱茏。夜晚，繁星点点，银河横贯天际。银河两岸，两颗明亮的星星遥遥相望：一颗是织女星，在中国象征着织女；另一颗是牛郎星，象征着牛郎。他们美丽的爱情故事世代相传。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Weaver Maid", "zh": "织女", "q": "This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated ", "tr": "七夕节时值盛夏，天气炎热，草木葱茏。夜晚，繁星点点，银河横贯天际。银河两岸，两颗明亮的星星遥遥相望：一颗是织女星，在中国象征着织女；另一颗是牛郎星，象征着牛郎。他们美丽的爱情故事世代相传。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Altair", "zh": "牛郎星", "q": "This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated ", "tr": "七夕节时值盛夏，天气炎热，草木葱茏。夜晚，繁星点点，银河横贯天际。银河两岸，两颗明亮的星星遥遥相望：一颗是织女星，在中国象征着织女；另一颗是牛郎星，象征着牛郎。他们美丽的爱情故事世代相传。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Cowhand", "zh": "牛郎", "q": "This festival falls in mid-summer, when the weather is hot and the grass and trees are at their greenest. At night, the sky is dotted with stars, and the Milky Way stretches across the sky. Separated ", "tr": "七夕节时值盛夏，天气炎热，草木葱茏。夜晚，繁星点点，银河横贯天际。银河两岸，两颗明亮的星星遥遥相望：一颗是织女星，在中国象征着织女；另一颗是牛郎星，象征着牛郎。他们美丽的爱情故事世代相传。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Queen Mother of the West", "zh": "西王母", "q": "A long time ago, there was an honest and kind-hearted man, the Cowhand, who led a simple life with only one companion—an old ox. One day, a fairy, the Weaver Maid, fell in love with him and secretly c", "tr": "很久以前，有一个忠厚善良的男子，人称牛郎，他生活简朴，只有一头老牛与他相伴。一天，仙女织女爱上了他，偷偷下凡与他成婚。牛郎在田间耕作，织女在家中织布，两人生活幸福，还养育了一儿一女。不料，西王母发现了他们的结合，便派天兵天将把织女带回天上。", "pos": "宗教", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "hairpins", "zh": "发簪", "q": "With the help of his ox, the Cowhand flew up to heaven with his son and daughter. Just as he was about to reach his wife, the Queen Mother of the West took off one of her hairpins (发簪) and drew a line", "tr": "在老牛的帮助下，牛郎挑着一双儿女飞上天去。就在他即将追上妻子时，西王母拔下头上的发簪，往空中一划，一条天河顿时出现。牛郎和织女被这条天河永远隔开，只能隔河洒泪。他们的爱情感动了喜鹊，喜鹊们纷纷飞来，搭成一座鹊桥，让他们得以相会。最终，西王母也被打动，允许他们每年农历七月初七相会一次。因此，他们相会的这一天便被称为\"七夕\"。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "magpies", "zh": "喜鹊", "q": "With the help of his ox, the Cowhand flew up to heaven with his son and daughter. Just as he was about to reach his wife, the Queen Mother of the West took off one of her hairpins (发簪) and drew a line", "tr": "在老牛的帮助下，牛郎挑着一双儿女飞上天去。就在他即将追上妻子时，西王母拔下头上的发簪，往空中一划，一条天河顿时出现。牛郎和织女被这条天河永远隔开，只能隔河洒泪。他们的爱情感动了喜鹊，喜鹊们纷纷飞来，搭成一座鹊桥，让他们得以相会。最终，西王母也被打动，允许他们每年农历七月初七相会一次。因此，他们相会的这一天便被称为\"七夕\"。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Civil Affairs Bureau", "zh": "民政局", "q": "Today, the Double Seventh Festival is regarded as Chinese Valentine's Day. On this day, more and more people go on dates and exchange gifts to express their affection. Long queues form at the gate of ", "tr": "如今，七夕节被视为中国的情人节。这一天，越来越多的人约会、互赠礼物，以表达爱意。当地民政局门前排起长队，情侣们争相前来登记结婚。这个节日传递着一种寓意：自主选择爱情，并忠贞不渝。", "pos": "制度", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "osmanthus flowers", "zh": "桂花", "q": "Most commonly enjoyed during the Mid-Autumn Festival, mooncakes are traditional Chinese pastries that symbolise reunion and harmony. Since the festival is also the time when sweet-scented osmanthus fl", "tr": "月饼是中秋节最常食用的中国传统糕点，象征着团圆与和睦。由于中秋节也正值桂花盛开之时，一边赏月，一边品尝桂花糕、饮用桂花酒，是节日中颇受欢迎的一项活动。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "chrysanthemums", "zh": "菊花", "q": "The celebration of the festival is vibrant and typically includes a variety of activities such as outings, mountain climbing, appreciating chrysanthemums (菊花), wearing dogwood (茱萸), eating Chongyang c", "tr": "重阳节的庆祝活动丰富多彩，通常包括出游、登高、赏菊、佩戴茱萸、吃重阳糕、饮菊花酒等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "dogwood", "zh": "茱萸", "q": "The celebration of the festival is vibrant and typically includes a variety of activities such as outings, mountain climbing, appreciating chrysanthemums (菊花), wearing dogwood (茱萸), eating Chongyang c", "tr": "重阳节的庆祝活动丰富多彩，通常包括出游、登高、赏菊、佩戴茱萸、吃重阳糕、饮菊花酒等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Seniors' Day", "zh": "老年节", "q": "In 2013, the Chinese government designated the ninth day of the ninth lunar month each year as the country's Seniors' Day (老年节), marking the official recognition of the Double Ninth Festival at the na", "tr": "2013年，中国政府将每年农历九月初九定为\"老年节\"，标志着重阳节在国家层面得到正式认可。政府部门、社会团体和社区常常组织老年人秋游，晚辈也常常陪伴家中长辈外出游玩，或送上礼物，以表敬意。", "pos": "制度", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Chongyang Cake", "zh": "重阳糕", "q": "The celebration of the festival is vibrant and typically includes a variety of activities such as outings, mountain climbing, appreciating chrysanthemums (菊花), wearing dogwood (茱萸), eating Chongyang c", "tr": "重阳节的庆祝活动丰富多彩，通常包括出游、登高、赏菊、佩戴茱萸、吃重阳糕、饮菊花酒等。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Water-Splashing Festival", "zh": "泼水节", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Nadam Fair", "zh": "那达慕", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Torch Festival", "zh": "火把节", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Danu Festival", "zh": "达努节", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Third Month Fair", "zh": "三月街节", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Gexu Singing Festival", "zh": "歌圩节", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Tibetan New Year", "zh": "藏历新年", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Flower-Jumping Festival", "zh": "跳花节", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Empty Day", "zh": "空日", "q": "The first day of the festival is the eve. On this day, the Dai people engage in activities such as rocket launching and dragon boat racing. The rockets are bamboo pipes filled with gunpowder, which ar", "tr": "泼水节的第一天是除夕。这一天，傣族人民举行放高升、赛龙舟等活动。所谓\"高升\"，是装满火药的竹筒，射向天空时会留下阵阵烟痕。第二天是\"空日\"，既不属于旧年，也不属于新年。第三天是新年第一天。人们换上最好的衣服，到寺庙用清水为佛像沐浴，并聆听佛经。仪式结束后，泼水狂欢便开始了，人们互相泼水，为新年祈福，祝愿彼此好运、富足、健康。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "antiphonal singing", "zh": "对歌", "q": "Danu means \"never forget\" in the Yao language. The Danu Festival commemorates the ancestor of the Yao ethnic group and also serves as the Yao people's New Year celebration. It begins on the twenty-nin", "tr": "达努在瑶语中意为\"永远不忘\"。达努节是为了纪念瑶族的祖先，同时也是瑶族的新年。它在农历五月二十九开始。节日当天，人们盛装打扮，品尝鸡肉、羊肉等菜肴，畅饮陈年米酒。节日期间还有铜鼓舞、对歌等娱乐活动。", "pos": "艺术", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "reed-pipe dance", "zh": "芦笙舞", "q": "The Flower-Jumping Festival is celebrated by the Miao people in Guizhou Province and other Miao-inhabited areas between the seventh and ninth days of the first lunar month. During this annual festival", "tr": "跳花节是贵州及苗族聚居地区的苗族人民在农历正月初七至初九庆祝的节日。每年这个时候，成千上万的苗族人聚集在开阔的山坡上，举行各种节庆活动，包括对歌、芦笙舞、斗牛，以及爬花杆比赛，比赛的冠军可获得一个猪头和上等美酒。", "pos": "艺术", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "the Twenty-Four Solar Terms", "zh": "二十四节气", "q": "the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology.", "tr": "二十四节气，是人们通过观察太阳的周年运动、认识一年中季节、气候与物候的变化而形成的知识体系和社会实践。它为农业生产提供了时间框架，于2016年被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。二十四节气依次为立春、雨水、惊蛰、春分、清明、谷雨、立夏、小满、芒种、夏至、小暑、大暑、立秋、处暑、白露、秋分、寒露、霜降、立冬、小雪、大雪、冬至、小寒、大寒。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "The Miao People Celebrating the Flower-Jumping Festival", "zh": "苗族人民庆祝跳花节", "q": "", "tr": "", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "pastures", "zh": "牧场", "q": "The Nadam Fair is a traditional festival observed by the Mongolian people. It takes place in July and August when the pastures (牧场) are rich in water, the grass is lush (茂盛的), the herds are thriving, ", "tr": "那达慕是蒙古族的传统节日。它在七八月间举行，此时牧场水草丰美、青草茂盛、牲畜肥壮、天气晴好。这一节日是一场盛大的娱乐集会，也是庆祝一年丰收的节日。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Spring Festival", "zh": "春节", "q": "③ How does your family celebrate the Spring Festival?", "tr": "③ 你家是怎样过春节的？", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "New Year's Eve", "zh": "除夕", "q": "In China, celebrating the Spring Festival is often referred to as guo nian, which literally means \"passing the year\". It symbolises bidding farewell to the old year and welcoming the new one. There ar", "tr": "在中国，过春节常被称为\"过年\"，其字面意思就是\"送走旧岁之年\"。它象征着辞别旧岁、迎接新年。关于\"年\"的起源，流传着许多传说。其中最著名的一个传说是：远古时期，中国曾生活着一头名叫\"年\"的凶猛怪兽。它常年深居海底，只在除夕这天爬上岸来，吞食牲畜和百姓。为了躲避它的袭击，村民们纷纷逃往偏远的深山。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Lantern Festival", "zh": "元宵节", "q": "The lively Spring Festival atmosphere not only fills homes but also spreads throughout streets and lanes. Activities such as lion dances, dragon dances, and temple fairs are held for days. Traditional", "tr": "热闹的春节气氛不仅充盈在千家万户，也弥漫在大街小巷。舞狮、舞龙、庙会等活动会持续好几天。按照传统，春节要到元宵节才算结束。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Qingming Festival", "zh": "清明节", "q": "The Qingming Festival, also known as Tomb-Sweeping Day, is a day for mourning (哀悼) the dead and honouring ancestors. It usually falls between 4th April and 6th April, the first day of the Pure Brightn", "tr": "清明节又称\"扫墓日\"，是哀悼逝者、祭祀祖先的日子。它通常在4月4日至6日之间，也就是二十四节气中\"清明\"节气的第一天，而清明正是中国传统历法二十四节气之一。清明前后，气温回升、降雨增多，标志着春耕和播种的开始。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Dragon Boat Festival", "zh": "端午节", "q": "The Dragon Boat Festival, also known as the Duanwu Festival, falls on the fifth day of the fifth lunar month and is celebrated throughout China. There are several legends describing the origins of the", "tr": "端午节又称龙舟节，在农历五月初五，中国各地都会庆祝。关于端午节的起源有多种传说，其中最流行的一种认为它是为了纪念屈原。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Double Seventh Festival", "zh": "七夕节", "q": "The Double Seventh Festival, celebrated on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, is a traditional festival full of romance.", "tr": "七夕节在农历七月初七，是一个充满浪漫色彩的传统节日。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Mid-Autumn Festival", "zh": "中秋节", "q": "The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month. The story below is one of the many legends about the festival.", "tr": "中秋节在农历八月十五。下面这则故事，便是关于中秋节的众多传说之一。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Double Ninth Festival", "zh": "重阳节", "q": "The Double Ninth Festival, or Chongyang Festival, is celebrated on the ninth day of the ninth lunar month. The festival dates back to the Warring States Period. According to The Book of Changes, the n", "tr": "重阳节在农历九月初九，可追溯至战国时期。据《易经》记载，数字\"九\"代表\"阳\"。由于月份和日期都逢\"九\"，便有了\"重阳\"之名，其中\"重\"意为\"重复\"。中国先民把\"两阳相重\"的这一天视为大吉之日，因此自远古起便有庆祝重阳节的习俗。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Xiaonian Festival", "zh": "小年", "q": "The timing of the Xiaonian Festival varies across China. In northern regions, it falls on the 23rd day of the 12th lunar month, while in southern China, it is celebrated on the 24th day. The Xiaonian ", "tr": "小年节的日期在中国各地有所不同。北方地区在农历十二月二十三，南方地区则过二十四。小年标志着春节筹备的开始。这一天，家家户户都要祭灶，供上甜食等祭品。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Laba Festival", "zh": "腊八节", "q": "In northern China, there is a saying: \"Right after laba, the new year is coming.\" The Laba Festival is generally considered the commencement of the Chinese New Year celebrations. It falls on the eight", "tr": "中国北方有句俗话：\"过了腊八就是年。\"腊八节一般被视为春节庆祝活动的开端，在农历十二月初八。其中\"腊\"指农历十二月，\"八\"即数字八，\"腊八\"之名便由此而来。这一天，人们喝腊八粥，感谢丰收，并祈求来年好运与健康。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "laba porridge", "zh": "腊八粥", "q": "In northern China, there is a saying: \"Right after laba, the new year is coming.\" The Laba Festival is generally considered the commencement of the Chinese New Year celebrations. It falls on the eight", "tr": "中国北方有句俗话：\"过了腊八就是年。\"腊八节一般被视为春节庆祝活动的开端，在农历十二月初八。其中\"腊\"指农历十二月，\"八\"即数字八，\"腊八\"之名便由此而来。这一天，人们喝腊八粥，感谢丰收，并祈求来年好运与健康。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "mooncakes", "zh": "月饼", "q": "Most commonly enjoyed during the Mid-Autumn Festival, mooncakes are traditional Chinese pastries that symbolise reunion and harmony. Since the festival is also the time when sweet-scented osmanthus fl", "tr": "月饼是中秋节最常食用的中国传统糕点，象征着团圆与和睦。由于中秋节也正值桂花盛开之时，一边赏月，一边品尝桂花糕、饮用桂花酒，是节日中颇受欢迎的一项活动。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "temple fair", "zh": "庙会", "q": "The lively Spring Festival atmosphere not only fills homes but also spreads throughout streets and lanes. Activities such as lion dances, dragon dances, and temple fairs are held for days. Traditional", "tr": "热闹的春节气氛不仅充盈在千家万户，也弥漫在大街小巷。舞狮、舞龙、庙会等活动会持续好几天。按照传统，春节要到元宵节才算结束。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "red envelope", "zh": "红包", "q": "On New Year's Eve, the younger generation extends greetings to their parents and other senior family members. Parents and the elders usually wrap brand-new banknotes with red paper or place them in re", "tr": "除夕之夜，晚辈要向父母和其他长辈拜年问好。父母和长辈通常把崭新的钞票用红纸包好，或装进红包，送给晚辈，这就是\"压岁钱\"。红色象征生机、喜庆和好运。人们相信，这一传统能为孩子带来来年健康成长、平安顺遂的祝福。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "lucky money", "zh": "压岁钱", "q": "On New Year's Eve, the younger generation extends greetings to their parents and other senior family members. Parents and the elders usually wrap brand-new banknotes with red paper or place them in re", "tr": "除夕之夜，晚辈要向父母和其他长辈拜年问好。父母和长辈通常把崭新的钞票用红纸包好，或装进红包，送给晚辈，这就是\"压岁钱\"。红色象征生机、喜庆和好运。人们相信，这一传统能为孩子带来来年健康成长、平安顺遂的祝福。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "dragon boat racing", "zh": "赛龙舟", "q": "Dragon boat racing is an indispensable part of the festival. Teams in dragon-shaped boats row to the beat of drums. It has developed into a sport that embodies both Chinese tradition and modern sports", "tr": "赛龙舟是端午节不可或缺的一部分。参赛队伍乘坐龙舟，伴着鼓点奋力划桨。赛龙舟已发展成一项融中国传统与现代体育精神于一体的运动。中国的赛龙舟从一项地方活动发展成为盛大的体育赛事，甚至走向国际，成为一项国际性的体育赛事。", "pos": "艺术", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "paper cutting", "zh": "剪纸", "q": "Decorations are an important feature of the Spring Festival celebrations. Traditional Chinese paper cuttings are pasted on windows. The paper used is usually red, and the designs may include birds, fl", "tr": "装饰是春节庆祝活动的重要特色。人们把传统的中国剪纸贴在窗上，所用纸张通常为红色，图案包括花鸟、鱼和风景等。家家户户还在大门上贴对联，对联用书法写成，象征着万象更新、春回大地。除了贴对联，人们还常张贴写有\"春\"\"财\"\"福\"等字的书法作品。", "pos": "艺术", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Kitchen God", "zh": "灶神", "q": "The timing of the Xiaonian Festival varies across China. In northern regions, it falls on the 23rd day of the 12th lunar month, while in southern China, it is celebrated on the 24th day. The Xiaonian ", "tr": "小年节的日期在中国各地有所不同。北方地区在农历十二月二十三，南方地区则过二十四。小年标志着春节筹备的开始。这一天，家家户户都要祭灶，供上甜食等祭品。", "pos": "宗教", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "firecrackers", "zh": "爆竹", "q": "Around midnight, the monster Nian rushed into the village. He found the atmosphere quite different from that of previous years. The grandmother's house at the edge of the village was brightly lit, wit", "tr": "时近午夜，年兽闯进村子，发觉气氛与往年大不相同。村口老婆婆的家中灯火通明，门上贴着鲜红的纸。年兽大惊，发出一声怪叫，恶狠狠地盯着这户人家。它刚走近门口，突然炸响的鞭炮声便震耳欲聋。年兽吓得浑身发抖，再也不敢向前迈进一步。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Moon Palace", "zh": "月宫", "q": "To reward his deeds, the Queen Mother of the West granted him an elixir of immortality. One day, while Yi was away hunting, his disciple Pang Meng (逢蒙) tried to seize the elixir. In desperation, Yi's ", "tr": "为奖赏他的功绩，西王母赐给他一丸长生不老药。一天，羿外出打猎，他的徒弟逢蒙企图抢夺仙药。情急之下，羿的妻子、温柔善良的嫦娥自己吞下了仙药，随即飘然升起，飞向月亮，在清冷寂静的月宫中开始了孤独的生活。", "pos": "术语", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Spring Festival Gala", "zh": "春节联欢晚会", "q": "After dinner on New Year's Eve, the whole family usually get together to chat and watch TV. Since 1983, watching the Spring Festival Gala has become an integral part of Chinese New Year celebrations f", "tr": "除夕饭后，全家人通常会聚在一起聊天、看电视。自1983年起，观看春节联欢晚会已成为海内外中国人欢度春节不可或缺的一部分。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Miluo River", "zh": "汨罗江", "q": "When Qu Yuan heard that Qin troops had finally conquered the Chu capital, he finished his final work, which was later included in Odes of Chu, and jumped into the Miluo River. It was the fifth day of ", "tr": "当屈原听说秦军攻陷了楚国都城，他写完了自己的最后一篇作品（后来收入《楚辞》），便纵身跳入汨罗江。这一天正是农历五月初五。", "pos": "地理", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "State of Chu", "zh": "楚国", "q": "Qu Yuan was a minister of the State of Chu during the Warring States Period and is recognised as China's first great patriotic poet. In the face of great pressure from the powerful State of Qin, he ad", "tr": "屈原是战国时期楚国的大臣，被公认为中国第一位伟大的爱国诗人。面对强秦的巨大压力，他主张富国强兵。然而，他的改革主张遭到贵族反对，后来先被楚怀王免职，又被楚顷襄王流放。即便身处流放之中，他依然心系国家和百姓，并把这种情怀寄托于诗篇。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "State of Qin", "zh": "秦国", "q": "Qu Yuan was a minister of the State of Chu during the Warring States Period and is recognised as China's first great patriotic poet. In the face of great pressure from the powerful State of Qin, he ad", "tr": "屈原是战国时期楚国的大臣，被公认为中国第一位伟大的爱国诗人。面对强秦的巨大压力，他主张富国强兵。然而，他的改革主张遭到贵族反对，后来先被楚怀王免职，又被楚顷襄王流放。即便身处流放之中，他依然心系国家和百姓，并把这种情怀寄托于诗篇。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Mount Kunlun", "zh": "昆仑山", "q": "In the distant past, there were 10 suns in the sky, whose scorching heat burnt all the crops, dried up all the lakes, and put people at death's door. A hero named Yi (羿) was deeply troubled by people'", "tr": "远古时候，天上有十个太阳，烈日炙烤，烧焦了庄稼，烤干了湖泽，把百姓逼到了死亡的边缘。一位名叫羿的英雄深深为百姓的苦难而忧虑。他登上昆仑山，拉开神弓，射下九个太阳，只留下一个造福人间。从此，他深受人们的敬重与爱戴。", "pos": "地理", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Odes of Chu", "zh": "楚辞", "q": "When Qu Yuan heard that Qin troops had finally conquered the Chu capital, he finished his final work, which was later included in Odes of Chu, and jumped into the Miluo River. It was the fifth day of ", "tr": "当屈原听说秦军攻陷了楚国都城，他写完了自己的最后一篇作品（后来收入《楚辞》），便纵身跳入汨罗江。这一天正是农历五月初五。", "pos": "作品名", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "The Book of Changes", "zh": "易经", "q": "The Double Ninth Festival, or Chongyang Festival, is celebrated on the ninth day of the ninth lunar month. The festival dates back to the Warring States Period. According to The Book of Changes, the n", "tr": "重阳节在农历九月初九，可追溯至战国时期。据《易经》记载，数字\"九\"代表\"阳\"。由于月份和日期都逢\"九\"，便有了\"重阳\"之名，其中\"重\"意为\"重复\"。中国先民把\"两阳相重\"的这一天视为大吉之日，因此自远古起便有庆祝重阳节的习俗。", "pos": "作品名", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Spring and Autumn Period", "zh": "春秋时期", "q": "The Hanshi Festival, or Cold Food Day (寒食节), is observed one or two days before the Qingming Festival. On this day, people traditionally refrain from using fire and eat only cold food. The custom comm", "tr": "寒食节在清明节的前一两天。这一天，按传统人们不生火做饭，只吃冷食。这一习俗是为了纪念春秋时期辅佐公子重耳的忠臣介子推。相传，介子推曾割下自己腿上的肉，给流亡中的主君充饥。后来重耳成为国君，曾放火烧山，想逼介子推回朝；介子推宁可与母亲一起隐居，最终死于大火。为悼念他的牺牲，这位国君下令在这一天禁止生火，寒食节便由此而来。", "pos": "朝代", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Warring States Period", "zh": "战国时期", "q": "Qu Yuan was a minister of the State of Chu during the Warring States Period and is recognised as China's first great patriotic poet. In the face of great pressure from the powerful State of Qin, he ad", "tr": "屈原是战国时期楚国的大臣，被公认为中国第一位伟大的爱国诗人。面对强秦的巨大压力，他主张富国强兵。然而，他的改革主张遭到贵族反对，后来先被楚怀王免职，又被楚顷襄王流放。即便身处流放之中，他依然心系国家和百姓，并把这种情怀寄托于诗篇。", "pos": "朝代", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Song Dynasty", "zh": "宋朝", "q": "Lanterns of various shapes and sizes are hung in the streets, attracting countless visitors. Excited children hold lanterns as they stroll (闲逛) along the streets. Guessing riddles pasted onto lanterns", "tr": "各式各样、大小不一的灯笼挂满街头，吸引来无数游人。兴奋的孩子们提着灯笼，在街上漫步。猜灯谜是宋朝流传下来的传统。猜中灯谜的游人还能获得奖品。", "pos": "朝代", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Mongolian", "zh": "蒙古族", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Tibetan", "zh": "藏族", "q": "China is a unified multi-ethnic country where festivals and celebrations vary from one ethnic group to another. The traditional festivals of China's ethnic minority groups include the Water-Splashing ", "tr": "中国是一个统一的多民族国家，各民族都有自己独特的节日和庆典。中国少数民族的传统节日包括傣族的泼水节、蒙古族的那达慕、彝族的火把节、瑶族的达努节、白族的三月街节、壮族的歌圩节、藏族的藏历新年，以及苗族的跳花节等。", "pos": "其他", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "UNESCO", "zh": "联合国教科文组织", "q": "the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology.", "tr": "二十四节气，是人们通过观察太阳的周年运动、认识一年中季节、气候与物候的变化而形成的知识体系和社会实践。它为农业生产提供了时间框架，于2016年被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。二十四节气依次为立春、雨水、惊蛰、春分、清明、谷雨、立夏、小满、芒种、夏至、小暑、大暑、立秋、处暑、白露、秋分、寒露、霜降、立冬、小雪、大雪、冬至、小寒、大寒。", "pos": "机构", "src": "原文"}, {"term": "Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity", "zh": "人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录", "q": "the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology.", "tr": "二十四节气，是人们通过观察太阳的周年运动、认识一年中季节、气候与物候的变化而形成的知识体系和社会实践。它为农业生产提供了时间框架，于2016年被列入联合国教科文组织人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。二十四节气依次为立春、雨水、惊蛰、春分、清明、谷雨、立夏、小满、芒种、夏至、小暑、大暑、立秋、处暑、白露、秋分、寒露、霜降、立冬、小雪、大雪、冬至、小寒、大寒。", "pos": "制度", "src": "原文"}], "vocabB": [], "examsA": [{"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "春节前夕吃团圆饭是中国人的传统。", "en": "Eating the reunion dinner on the eve of the Spring Festival is a Chinese tradition.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "团圆饭", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:动名词主语(→Eating the reunion dinner)(CL-02)|形式主语(It is…);文化:春节前夕→the Spring Festival Eve；团圆饭→S1 reunion dinner(CGTN/China Daily，定译趋势)", "ta": "动名词主语（\"吃团圆饭\"→Eating the reunion dinner，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合。", "te": "词性转换（\"中国人的传统\"→a Chinese tradition）；介词化（\"春节前夕\"→on the eve of the Spring Festival）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"团圆饭\"见 CGTN/China Daily（reunion dinner），S1。", "rp": "`on the eve of`（L2·PC）；`reunion dinner`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "团圆饭是一年中最重要的晚餐，也是家庭团聚的最佳时机，家人生活在不同地方的家庭尤其如此。", "en": "The reunion dinner is the most important dinner of the year and the best time for family reunions, especially for families whose members live in different places.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "团圆饭", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②/③;核心=①;②→并列(and);③→状语从句(especially for families whose members live in different places)|省略句;话题:补主语(→The reunion dinner);文化:团圆饭→S1 reunion dinner；家庭团聚→S1 family reunions(新华·央视)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；定语从句（whose…，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（especially）；词性转换（\"团聚\"→reunions）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"团圆饭\"见 CGTN/China Daily，S1；\"家庭团聚\"见 新华·央视（family reunions），S1。", "rp": "`the best time for`（L2·PC）；`families whose members …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：families whose members live in different places。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "团圆饭上的菜看丰富多样，其中有些菜看有特殊含义。", "en": "The dishes at the reunion dinner are rich and varied, and some of them have special meanings.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "团圆饭", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→定语从句(some of which have…)|并列(and);话题:补主语(→The dishes);文化:团圆饭→S1 reunion dinner；备注:[原卷如此]（\"菜看\"2 处疑为\"菜肴\"之误，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（定语从句 some of which / and，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "词性转换（\"丰富多样\"→rich and varied）；增补（of them）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"团圆饭\"见 CGTN/China Daily，S1。", "rp": "`some of which …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：some of which have special meanings。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "例如，鱼是不可缺少的一道莱，因为汉语中的“鱼”宇和“余”宇听上去一样。", "en": "For example, fish is an indispensable dish, because the Chinese characters for \"fish\" and \"surplus\" sound the same.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "团圆饭", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→原因状语(because…);逻辑:因果→because|as;话题:补主语(→fish);文化:年年有余(鱼/余谐音)→S1 may you have surplus year after year(CGTN/China Daily，本句未直出，作义项提示)；备注:[原卷如此]（\"一道莱\"疑\"一道菜\"之误、\"宇\"2 处疑\"字\"之误，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（因果 because，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"听上去一样\"→sound the same）；词性转换（\"一道菜\"→a dish）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"年年有余\"之谐音义对应 CGTN/China Daily（may you have surplus year after year），S1（本句未直出该短语，仅作义项提示）。", "rp": "`sound the same`（L2·PC）；`because …`（L2·SB，因果）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国的许多地方，饺子也是一道重要的佳肴，因为饺子象征着财富和好运。", "en": "In many parts of China, jiaozi is also an important dish, because it symbolizes wealth and good luck.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "团圆饭", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→原因状语(because…);逻辑:因果→because;话题:补主语(→jiaozi / dumplings);文化:饺子→jiaozi|dumplings；象征→S1 symbolize(China Daily HK)；好运→S1 good luck(新华社英文)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（因果 because）；地点状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词\"象征\"→symbolizes；音译（饺子→jiaozi）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"象征\"见 China Daily（symbolize），S1；\"好运\"见 新华社英文（good luck），S1。", "rp": "`symbolize wealth and good luck`（L2·PC）；`in many parts of China`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "，鱼是春节前夕餐桌上不可或缺的一道莱，因为汉语中“鱼”字的发音与“余”字的发音相同。", "en": "Fish is an indispensable dish on the dinner table on the eve of the Spring Festival, because the pronunciation of the Chinese character for \"fish\" is the same as that of \"surplus\".", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "鱼（年年有余）", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→原因状语(because…);逻辑:因果→because;话题:补主语(→fish);文化:春节前夕→the Spring Festival Eve；年年有余(鱼/余谐音)→S1 may you have surplus year after year(本句未直出，义项提示)；备注:[原卷如此]（句首残留半角逗号\"，\"、'一道莱'疑'一道菜'之误，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（因果 because，连淑能 1993）；句首异常标点功能删略（原文照抄另存）。", "te": "增补（the dinner table）；惯用等值（\"发音相同\"→the pronunciation … is the same as）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"年年有余\"之谐音义对应 CGTN/China Daily，S1（本句未直出该短语，仅作义项提示）。", "rp": "`the same as`（L2·PC）；`on the eve of`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "正由于这个象征性的意义，春节期间鱼也作为礼物送给亲戚朋友。", "en": "Because of this symbolic meaning, fish is also given as a gift to relatives and friends during the Spring Festival.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "鱼（年年有余）", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=②;①→原因状语(Because of this symbolic meaning…);逻辑:因果→because of;话题:受事→被动(→fish is given as a gift);文化:象征意义→symbolic meaning；春节→S1 the Spring Festival(定译)；亲友→relatives and friends", "ta": "受事→被动（is given as a gift，CL-04）；状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"正由于\"→Because of）；名词化（\"象征性的意义\"→symbolic meaning）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1。", "rp": "`be given as a gift`（L2·PC）；`because of`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "鱼的象征意义据说源于中国传统文化。", "en": "The symbolic meaning of fish is said to originate from traditional Chinese culture.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "鱼（年年有余）", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→the symbolic meaning of fish)|形式主语(It is said…);文化:中国传统文化→S1 traditional Chinese culture(China Daily)", "ta": "补主语；受事→被动（is said to，CL-04）。", "te": "动词\"源于\"→originate from；名词化（\"象征意义\"→symbolic meaning）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"中国传统文化\"见 China Daily（traditional Chinese culture），S1。", "rp": "`be said to`（L2·PC）；`originate from`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中国人有节省的传统，他们认为节省得愈多，就感到愈为安全。", "en": "Chinese people have a tradition of thrift: they believe that the more they save, the safer they feel.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "鱼（年年有余）", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②③→宾语从句/并列(and they believe that…);逻辑:①→主句，②③→补充；话题:补主语(→Chinese people);文化:节省→thrift|frugality；传统→tradition", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（冒号＋宾语从句，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"愈…愈…\"→the more … the more …）；词性转换（\"节省\"→thrift / save）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。", "rp": "`have a tradition of`（L2·PC）；`the more …, the more …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：the more they save, the safer they feel。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "今天，尽管人们愈来愈富裕了，但他们仍然认为节省是一种值得弘扬的美德。", "en": "Today, although people are getting richer and richer, they still regard thrift as a virtue worth promoting.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "鱼（年年有余）", "set": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2020·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=②;①→让步状语(Although…)|Despite…;逻辑:让步→although;时锚:今天→一般现在时|现在完成时;话题:补主语(→people);文化:传统美德→S3 traditional virtues(2026.06)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（让步 although，连淑能 1993）；时间状语前置。", "te": "增补（still）；动词短语（\"值得弘扬\"→worth promoting）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"传统美德\"库内作 traditional virtues，S3（非官方参考，供对照）。", "rp": "`although …, still …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：although people are getting richer and richer, they still regard …；`a virtue worth promoting`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "按照中国民间的传统习俗，春节期间长辈通常会给孩子发红包，俗称发压岁钱(luckymoney),以表达对孩子的祝福，祝他们好运。", "en": "According to traditional Chinese folk customs, during the Spring Festival elders usually give children red envelopes, commonly known as \"lucky money\", to express their blessings and wish them good luck.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红包（压岁钱）", "set": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "src": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②/③/④;核心=①;②→同位语(俗称…)|定语从句;③→目的状语(to express…)(CL-07);④→伴随/并列;话题:补主语(→elders);文化:红包→S1 red envelopes(China Daily Asia)；压岁钱→lucky money(原卷括注 luckymoney，同源优先，补空格)；春节→S1 the Spring Festival；备注:[原卷如此]（\"(luckymoney),\"为半角逗号紧接括注，照抄不改）", "ta": "补主语；目的状语化（to express，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合。", "te": "惯用等值（\"俗称\"→commonly known as）；增补（their）；词性转换（\"祝福\"→blessings）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"红包\"见 China Daily Asia（red envelopes），S1；\"压岁钱\"从原卷括注 luckymoney，取官配 lucky money。", "rp": "`commonly known as`（L2·PC）；`wish sb good luck`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "如今，红包不仅是给孩子的礼物，而且经常也是给长辈或亲朋好友的礼物。", "en": "Today, red envelopes are not only gifts for children but also gifts for elders or relatives and friends.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红包（压岁钱）", "set": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "src": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;触发[不仅…而且…]→not only … but also …;时锚:如今→一般现在时;话题:补主语(→red envelopes);文化:红包→S1 red envelopes；长辈→elders；亲朋好友→relatives and friends", "ta": "补主语；触发构式（not only … but also …，认知构式翻译观）；意合→形合。", "te": "增补（Today）；词性转换（\"礼物\"→gifts）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"红包\"见 China Daily Asia（red envelopes），S1。", "rp": "`not only … but also …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：red envelopes are not only gifts for children but also gifts for elders。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "近年来，随着微信用户数量的增加，微信红包变得愈加流行。", "en": "In recent years, with the increase in the number of WeChat users, WeChat red envelopes have become increasingly popular.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红包（压岁钱）", "set": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "src": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;①→伴随状语(With the increase in the number of WeChat users…)|时间从句;触发[随着…的增加]→with the increase in…|as…increases;时锚:近年来→现在完成时;话题:补主语(→WeChat red envelopes);文化:微信→WeChat(S4 未定位库内定译)", "ta": "补主语；状语前置（连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词短语（\"变得愈加流行\"→become increasingly popular）；增补（In recent years）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"微信\"无库内官方定译，标 S4。", "rp": "`with the increase in`（L2·PC）；`become increasingly popular`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "欢度春节时，人们经常互发微信红包表达问候。", "en": "When celebrating the Spring Festival, people often send each other WeChat red envelopes to express greetings.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红包（压岁钱）", "set": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "src": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→目的状语(to express…)|伴随;逻辑:目的→to V;时锚:经常→一般现在时;话题:补主语(→people);文化:微信红包→WeChat red envelopes；问候→greetings", "ta": "补主语；时间状语从句化（When V-ing…，连淑能 1993）；目的状语化（to express）。", "te": "增补（often / each other）；词性转换（\"问候\"→greetings）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。", "rp": "`send each other`（L2·PC）；`When V-ing …, S V …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：When celebrating the Spring Festival, people often send …。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "这无疑是一种与远方亲友联系的便捷方式。", "en": "This is undoubtedly a convenient way to keep in touch with relatives and friends far away.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红包（压岁钱）", "set": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "src": "CET4 2022·09·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→This is);文化:便捷→S1 convenient(People's Daily)；亲友→relatives and friends", "ta": "补主语；修饰语后置（far away，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"与…联系\"→keep in touch with）；增补（undoubtedly）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"便捷\"库内作 convenient，S1（People's Daily）。", "rp": "`a convenient way to V`（L2·PC）；`keep in touch with`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国农历中，立秋(Start of Autumn)意味着夏天的结束和秋天的开始。", "en": "In the Chinese lunar calendar, Start of Autumn means the end of summer and the beginning of autumn.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "立秋", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→Start of Autumn);文化:立秋→S1 Start of Autumn(原卷括注，同源优先；China Daily 并行定名)；农历→S1 the lunar calendar", "ta": "补主语；状语前置（连淑能 1993）；名词化（\"结束/开始\"→the end / the beginning，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "同源引用（Start of Autumn 取原卷括注）；名词化（\"开始\"→the beginning）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"立秋\"从原卷括注 Start of Autumn（China Daily 并行定名），S1；\"农历\"见 中国网（the lunar calendar），S1。", "rp": "`the end of … and the beginning of …`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "立秋带来的首先是天气的变化，气温逐渐下降。", "en": "What Start of Autumn brings first is a change in the weather, as temperatures gradually drop.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "立秋", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列(and)/独立分句;逻辑:并列→and|as;话题:补主语(→Start of Autumn / temperatures);文化:立秋→S1 Start of Autumn；气温→temperature", "ta": "补主语；主语从句化（What…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（as）。", "te": "增补（first）；名词化（\"变化\"→a change）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"立秋\"从原卷括注 Start of Autumn，S1。", "rp": "`a change in`（L2·PC）；`What … brings is …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：What Start of Autumn brings first is a change in the weather。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "人们看到树叶开始变黄飘落时，知道秋天已经来临，这就是所谓的“一叶知秋”。", "en": "When people see the leaves begin to turn yellow and fall, they know autumn has arrived; this is the so-called \"one falling leaf heralds the coming of autumn\".", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "立秋", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=②;①→时间状语从句(When…);③→总结/定语(which is called…);话题:补主语(→people);文化:一叶知秋→S4 One falling leaf heralds the coming of autumn(无官方定译)", "ta": "补主语；时间状语从句化（When…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（分号并句）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"一叶知秋\"→one falling leaf heralds the coming of autumn）；增补（so-called）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"一叶知秋\"为成语，无官方定译，标 S4 通译。", "rp": "`see sb/sth do`（L2·PC）；`the so-called …`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "但此时酷热的天气并未完全结束，高温通常还会持续一段时间，被称为“秋老虎”。", "en": "But the hot weather is not yet completely over; the high temperatures usually last for some time and are known as the \"Autumn Tiger\".", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "立秋", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②→并列;③→被动/定语(which is called…);逻辑:转折→but;话题:补主语(→the hot weather / the high temperatures);文化:秋老虎→S1 the \"Autumn Tiger\"(China Daily 定名)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（转折 but，连淑能 1993）；受事→被动（are known as，CL-04）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"秋老虎\"→the \"Autumn Tiger\"）；增补（usually / some）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"秋老虎\"见 China Daily 定名（the \"Autumn Tiger\"），S1。", "rp": "`be known as`（L2·PC）；`last for`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "立秋对农民意义重大，这时各种秋季作物迅速生长、开始成熟，收获的季节即将到来。", "en": "Start of Autumn is of great significance to farmers; at this time, various autumn crops grow rapidly and begin to ripen, and the harvest season is approaching.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "立秋", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②③→并列/独立分句;逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→Start of Autumn / autumn crops);文化:立秋→S1 Start of Autumn；收获→S1 harvest|S1 a good harvest", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；名词化（\"意义重大\"→is of great significance）。", "te": "词性转换（\"收获的季节\"→the harvest season）；增补（various）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"立秋\"从原卷括注 Start of Autumn，S1。", "rp": "`be of great significance to`（L2·PC）；`be approaching`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "冬至(Winter Solstice)是全年白昼最短、黑夜最长的一天，标志着一年中最寒冷时节的开始。", "en": "Winter Solstice is the shortest day and the longest night of the year, marking the beginning of the coldest season of the year.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "冬至", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→伴随状语(分词 marking…)(CL-03)|定语;话题:补主语(→Winter Solstice);文化:冬至→S1 Winter Solstice(原卷括注，同源优先；China Daily)", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（连淑能 1993）；意合→形合。", "te": "动词\"标志着\"→marking；名词化（\"开始\"→the beginning）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"冬至\"从原卷括注 Winter Solstice（China Daily），S1。", "rp": "`the shortest day and the longest night of the year`（L2·PC）；`V-ing 伴随`（L2·SB）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "冬至过后，气温越来越低，人们的户外活动逐渐减少。", "en": "After Winter Solstice, temperatures drop lower and lower, and people's outdoor activities gradually decrease.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "冬至", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=并列;逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→temperatures / people's outdoor activities);文化:冬至→S1 Winter Solstice；户外活动→S1 outdoor activities(深圳市政府英文站)", "ta": "补主语；时间状语前置（连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（and）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"越来越低/逐渐减少\"→drop lower and lower / gradually decrease）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"户外活动\"见 深圳市政府英文站（outdoor activities），S1；\"冬至\"从原卷括注 Winter Solstice，S1。", "rp": "`outdoor activities`（L2·PC）；`gradually decrease`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "农民地里活儿不多，主要忙于灌溉系统的维护和农作物的防冻，同时为来年春天播种做准备。", "en": "Farmers have little work in the fields; they are mainly busy with the maintenance of irrigation systems and the frost protection of crops, and are also preparing for spring sowing the following year.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "冬至", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②③→并列/目的;逻辑:并列→and|while;话题:补主语(→farmers);文化:灌溉系统→S1 irrigation systems(库内条 intelligent irrigation systems，CGTN 原型)；防冻→frost protection(S4)；播种→sowing", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（分号＋and，连淑能 1993）；名词化（\"维护/防冻/播种\"→maintenance / protection / sowing，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "词性转换（\"忙于\"→be busy with）；增补（mainly / the following year）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"灌溉系统\"库内作 intelligent irrigation systems（CGTN），S1（本句取原型 irrigation systems）；\"防冻\"\"播种\"无官方定译，S4。", "rp": "`be busy with`（L2·PC）；`prepare for`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中国人历来很重视冬至，许多地方都把冬至当作一个节日，庆祝方式各地不尽相同。", "en": "Chinese people have always attached great importance to Winter Solstice; in many places it is treated as a festival, and the ways of celebration vary from place to place.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "冬至", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②③→并列;逻辑:并列→and;时锚:历来→现在完成时|一般现在时;话题:补主语(→Chinese people);文化:冬至→S1 Winter Solstice；节日→festival", "ta": "补主语；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）；受事→被动（is treated as，CL-04）；意合→形合（分号＋and）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"重视\"→attach great importance to；\"不尽相同\"→vary from place to place）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"冬至\"从原卷括注 Winter Solstice，S1。", "rp": "`attach great importance to`（L2·PC）；`vary from place to place`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "北方人有冬至吃饺子(jiaozi)的习俗，南方人有冬至吃汤圆(tangyuan)的传统。", "en": "People in the north have the custom of eating jiaozi on Winter Solstice, while people in the south have the tradition of eating tangyuan.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "冬至", "set": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "src": "CET4 2022·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=并列;逻辑:并列对照→while|and;话题:补主语(→people in the north / the south);文化:冬至→S1 Winter Solstice；饺子→jiaozi(原卷括注，同源优先)；汤圆→tangyuan(原卷括注，同源优先)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（对照 while，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "同源引用（jiaozi / tangyuan 取原卷括注）；词性转换（\"习俗/传统\"→the custom / the tradition）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"冬至\"从原卷括注 Winter Solstice，S1；\"饺子\"jiaozi / \"汤圆\"tangyuan 取原卷括注（同源优先）。", "rp": "`have the custom of V-ing`（L2·PC）；`while …`（对照，L2·SB）→ 本句实例：… while people in the south have the tradition of eating tangyuan。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "农历 (the lunar calendar) 起源于数千年前的中国，根据太阳和月亮的运行规律制定。", "en": "The lunar calendar originated in China thousands of years ago and was formulated according to the movements of the sun and the moon.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "农历", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→伴随状语(分词 based on…)|受事→被动(was formulated);话题:补主语(→the lunar calendar);文化:农历→S1 the lunar calendar(原卷括注，同源优先；中国网/en.chinaculture)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and）；受事→被动（was formulated，CL-04）。", "te": "动词\"起源于\"→originated in；名词化（\"运行规律\"→the movements）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"农历\"从原卷括注 the lunar calendar（中国网/en.chinaculture），S1。", "rp": "`originate in`（L2·PC）；`according to`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "长期以来，农历在农业生产和人们日常生活中发挥着重要作用。", "en": "For a long time, the lunar calendar has played an important role in agricultural production and people's daily life.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "农历", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:长期以来→现在完成时|一般现在时;话题:补主语(→the lunar calendar);文化:农历→S1 the lunar calendar；农业生产→agricultural production", "ta": "补主语；时间状语前置；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"发挥重要作用\"→play an important role in）；增补（a long）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"农历\"从原卷括注 the lunar calendar，S1。", "rp": "`play an important role in`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "古人依据农历记录日期、安排农活，以便最有效地利用自然资源和气候条件，提高农作物的产量和质量。", "en": "The ancients recorded dates and arranged farm work according to the lunar calendar so as to make the most effective use of natural resources and climatic conditions and improve the yield and quality of crops.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "农历", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=①;②③→目的状语(CL-07);触发[以便]→so as to V|in order to V|to V;话题:补主语(→the ancients|ancient people);文化:农历→S1 the lunar calendar", "ta": "补主语；目的状语化（so as to，CL-07，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词并列（\"记录/安排\"→recorded / arranged）；名词化（\"产量和质量\"→the yield and quality）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"农历\"从原卷括注 the lunar calendar，S1。", "rp": "`so as to V`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：… so as to make the most effective use of natural resources…；`make use of`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中国的春节、中秋节等传统节日的日期都基于农历。", "en": "The dates of traditional Chinese festivals such as the Spring Festival and the Mid-Autumn Festival are all based on the lunar calendar.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "农历", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:受事→主语(the dates of…)|补主语;文化:春节→S1 the Spring Festival(定译)；中秋节→S1 the Mid-Autumn Festival(定译)；农历→S1 the lunar calendar", "ta": "补主语（受事→主语）；意合→形合（such as）。", "te": "增补（all）；词性转换（\"日期\"→the dates of）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1；\"中秋节\"官方定译 the Mid-Autumn Festival，S1；\"农历\"见 中国网，S1。", "rp": "`such as`（L2·PC）；`be based on`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "农历是中国传统文化的重要组成部分，当今依然广为使用。", "en": "The lunar calendar is an important part of traditional Chinese culture and is still widely used today.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "农历", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列(and);逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→the lunar calendar);文化:农历→S1 the lunar calendar；中国传统文化→S1 traditional Chinese culture(China Daily)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "词性转换（\"广为使用\"→widely used，被动）；增补（still / today）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"中国传统文化\"见 China Daily（traditional Chinese culture），S1；\"农历\"从原卷括注 the lunar calendar，S1。", "rp": "`an important part of`（L2·PC）；`widely used`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "汉语中的“福”字 (the character fu) 表示幸福和好运，是中国传统文化中最常用的吉祥 (auspicious)符号之一。", "en": "The Chinese character \"fu\" in Chinese means happiness and good luck, and is one of the most commonly used auspicious symbols in traditional Chinese culture.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "福字", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列(and);逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→the character \"fu\");文化:福字→the Chinese character \"fu\"(原卷括注 the character fu，同源优先；库内 2019.12 C4-3 统一式)；幸福→S1 happiness；好运→S1 good luck(新华社英文)；吉祥→S1 auspicious|good fortune；中国传统文化→S1 traditional Chinese culture", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and）；修饰语后置（in traditional Chinese culture，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "同源引用（the character \"fu\"）；增补（commonly）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"幸福\"见 China Daily（happiness），S1；\"好运\"见 新华社英文（good luck），S1；\"中国传统文化\"见 China Daily（traditional Chinese culture），S1；\"福\"字取原卷括注 the character fu。", "rp": "`one of the most commonly used …`（L2·PC）；`in traditional Chinese culture`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "人们通常将一个大大的福字写在红纸上，寓意期盼家庭幸福、社会安定、国家昌盛。", "en": "People usually write a big \"fu\" character on red paper, symbolizing the hope for family happiness, social stability and national prosperity.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "福字", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→伴随状语(分词 symbolizing…)|定语;话题:补主语(→people);文化:福字→the Chinese character \"fu\"；家庭幸福→family happiness；社会安定→social stability；国家昌盛→national prosperity", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（symbolizing…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合。", "te": "动词\"寓意/期盼\"→symbolize / the hope for；增补（big / national）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"福\"字承前取 the Chinese character \"fu\"。", "rp": "`write … on …`（L2·PC）；`the hope for`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "春节贴福字是民间由来已久的习俗。", "en": "Pasting the \"fu\" character during the Spring Festival is a long-standing folk custom.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "福字", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:动名词主语(→Pasting the \"fu\" character)(CL-02);文化:春节→S1 the Spring Festival(定译)；福字→the Chinese character \"fu\"；习俗→custom", "ta": "动名词主语（\"贴福字\"→Pasting the \"fu\" character，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"由来已久\"→long-standing）；介词化（\"春节\"→during the Spring Festival）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1。", "rp": "`a long-standing custom`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "为了欢庆春节，家家户户都会将福字贴在门上或墙上，表达对幸福生活的向往、对美好未来的期待。", "en": "To celebrate the Spring Festival, every household pastes the \"fu\" character on the door or wall, expressing their yearning for a happy life and their expectation of a bright future.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "福字", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;①→目的状语(To celebrate…)(CL-07);②→伴随状语(分词 expressing…)|目的;话题:补主语(→every household);文化:春节→S1 the Spring Festival；幸福生活→a happy life；对美好生活的向往→S1 yearning for a better life(China Daily Asia，义近)", "ta": "补主语；目的状语前置（To V…，CL-07，连淑能 1993）；分词状语（expressing…）。", "te": "动词\"向往/期待\"→yearning / expectation；增补（their / bright）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1；\"对美好生活的向往\"义近 China Daily Asia（yearning for a better life），S1。", "rp": "`yearning for a better life`（L2·PC）；`expectation of`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "人们有时还将福字倒过来贴，表示幸福已到、好运已到。", "en": "People sometimes paste the \"fu\" character upside down, indicating that good fortune and good luck have arrived.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "福字", "set": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "src": "CET4 2024·06·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→伴随状语(分词 indicating…)|目的;话题:补主语(→people);文化:福字→the Chinese character \"fu\"；倒贴→S1 paste it upside down(中国网)；福气到了→S1 good fortune has arrived(中国网)", "ta": "补主语；分词状语（indicating…，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"倒过来贴\"→paste … upside down）；增补（sometimes）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"倒贴\"见 中国网（paste it upside down），S1；\"福气到了\"见 中国网（good fortune has arrived），S1。", "rp": "`upside down`（L2·PC）；`have arrived`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "灯笼起源于东汉，最初主要用于照明。", "en": "Lanterns originated in the Eastern Han Dynasty and were initially used mainly for illumination.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "灯笼（红灯笼）", "set": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列/时间补充;逻辑:并列→and;时锚:起源于/最初→一般过去时;话题:补主语(→lanterns);文化:灯笼→S1 lantern(China Daily Asia)；东汉→S4 the Eastern Han Dynasty(未定位官方直配)；照明→S1 illumination|lighting(China Daily Asia)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；受事→被动（were used for，CL-04）。", "te": "动词\"起源于\"→originated in；惯用等值（\"用于\"→be used for）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"灯笼\"见 China Daily Asia（lantern），S1；\"照明\"见 China Daily Asia（illumination），S1；\"东汉\"库内未定位官方英文直配，标 S4。", "rp": "`originate in`（L2·PC）；`be used for`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在唐代，人们用红灯笼来庆祝安定的生活。", "en": "In the Tang Dynasty, people used red lanterns to celebrate a stable life.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "灯笼（红灯笼）", "set": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:唐代→一般过去时;话题:补主语(→people);文化:唐代→S1 the Tang Dynasty(China Daily Asia)；红灯笼→S1 red lanterns(China Daily Global/新华)；庆祝安定的生活→S4 celebrate a stable life", "ta": "补主语；时间状语前置；目的状语化（to celebrate）。", "te": "增补（the）；词性转换（\"安定的生活\"→a stable life）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"唐代\"见 China Daily Asia（the Tang Dynasty），S1；\"红灯笼\"见 China Daily/新华（red lanterns），S1；\"安定的生活\"未定位官方英文直配，标 S4。", "rp": "`use … to V`（L2·PC）；`celebrate a stable life`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "从那时起，灯笼在中国的许多地方流行起来。", "en": "From then on, lanterns became popular in many parts of China.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "灯笼（红灯笼）", "set": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:从那时起→一般过去时|现在完成时;话题:补主语(→lanterns);文化:灯笼→S1 lantern", "ta": "补主语；时间状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词短语（\"流行起来\"→became popular）；增补（From then on）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"灯笼\"见 China Daily Asia（lantern），S1。", "rp": "`become popular`（L2·PC）；`from then on`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "灯笼通常用色彩鲜艳的薄纸，形状和尺寸各异。", "en": "Lanterns are usually made of thin, brightly colored paper and come in various shapes and sizes.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "灯笼（红灯笼）", "set": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列(and);逻辑:并列→and;话题:受事→被动(→lanterns are usually made of…)|补主语;文化:灯笼→S1 lantern；形状和尺寸各异→S1 various shapes and sizes(China Daily Asia)", "ta": "受事→被动（are made of，CL-04）；意合→形合（and）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"形状和尺寸各异\"→come in various shapes and sizes）；增补（thin）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"灯笼\"见 China Daily Asia（lantern），S1；\"形状和尺寸各异\"见 China Daily Asia（various shapes and sizes），S1。", "rp": "`be made of`（L2·PC）；`come in various shapes and sizes`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国传统文化中，红灯笼象征生活美满和生意兴隆，通常在春节、元宵节和国庆等节日期间悬挂。", "en": "In traditional Chinese culture, red lanterns symbolize a happy life and a thriving business, and are usually hung during festivals such as the Spring Festival, the Lantern Festival and National Day.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "灯笼（红灯笼）", "set": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→被动/伴随(are usually hung…);逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→red lanterns);受事→被动(通常…悬挂→are usually hung);文化:中国传统文化→S1 traditional Chinese culture；红灯笼→S1 red lanterns；春节→S1 the Spring Festival；元宵节→S1 the Lantern Festival(China Daily Asia)；国庆→National Day", "ta": "补主语；受事→被动（are hung，CL-04）；意合→形合（and）；状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"生意兴隆\"→a thriving business）；增补（usually / such as）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"红灯笼\"见 China Daily/新华（red lanterns），S1；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1；\"元宵节\"见 China Daily Asia（the Lantern Festival），S1；\"中国传统文化\"见 China Daily（traditional Chinese culture），S1。", "rp": "`symbolize … and …`（L2·PC）；`be hung during`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "如今，世界上许多其他地方也能看到红灯笼。", "en": "Today, red lanterns can also be seen in many other parts of the world.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "灯笼（红灯笼）", "set": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "src": "CET4 2019·06·S2", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:如今→一般现在时;话题:受事→被动(→red lanterns can be seen…)|形式主语;文化:红灯笼→S1 red lanterns", "ta": "受事→被动（can be seen，CL-04）；时间状语前置；地点状语后置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "增补（Today / also）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"红灯笼\"见 China Daily/新华（red lanterns），S1。", "rp": "`can be seen in`（L2·PC）；`many other parts of the world`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在中国文化中，红色通常象征着好运、长寿和幸福。", "en": "In Chinese culture, red usually symbolizes good luck, longevity and happiness.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红色", "set": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→red);文化:好运→S1 good luck(新华社英文)；幸福→S1 happiness(China Daily)；长寿→S4 longevity(未定位官方同形)", "ta": "补主语；状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词\"象征\"→symbolizes；增补（usually）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"好运\"见 新华社英文（good luck），S1；\"幸福\"见 China Daily（happiness），S1；\"长寿\"未定位官方同形，标 S4。", "rp": "`symbolize good luck, longevity and happiness`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "在春节和其他喜庆场合，红色到处可见。", "en": "During the Spring Festival and other festive occasions, red can be seen everywhere.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红色", "set": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→red)|受事→被动;文化:春节→S1 the Spring Festival(定译)；喜庆场合→festive occasions", "ta": "受事→被动（can be seen，CL-04）；状语前置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"到处可见\"→can be seen everywhere）；增补（other）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1。", "rp": "`be seen everywhere`（L2·PC）；`festive occasions`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "人们把现金作为礼物送给家人或亲密朋友时，通常放在红信封里。", "en": "When people give cash as a gift to family members or close friends, it is usually put in a red envelope.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红色", "set": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→伴随/结果(→it is usually put in a red envelope);话题:补主语(→people);受事→被动(现金被放在红信封里);文化:红信封→a red envelope(义近库内 red envelopes，China Daily Asia)", "ta": "补主语；时间状语从句化（When…，连淑能 1993）；受事→被动（is put，CL-04）。", "te": "增补（close）；词性转换（\"现金\"→cash）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"红包\"义近库内 red envelopes（China Daily Asia），S1。", "rp": "`give sth as a gift`（L2·PC）；`a red envelope`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "红色在中国流行的另一个原因是人们把它与中国革命和共产党相联系。", "en": "Another reason why red is popular in China is that people associate it with the Chinese revolution and the Communist Party.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红色", "set": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→表语从句(that…);话题:补主语(→Another reason);文化:中国革命→the Chinese revolution；共产党→the Communist Party(义近官方专名 the Communist Party of China/CPC，gov.cn/SCIO)", "ta": "补主语；定语从句＋表语从句（why… / that…，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"把…与…相联系\"→associate … with …）；增补（Another / Chinese）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"共产党\"义近官方专名 the Communist Party of China（CPC，gov.cn/SCIO），S1。", "rp": "`associate A with B`（L2·PC）；`another reason why …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：Another reason why red is popular in China is that …。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "然而，红色并不总是代表好运和快乐。", "en": "However, red does not always represent good luck and happiness.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红色", "set": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;逻辑:转折→However;话题:补主语(→red);文化:好运→S1 good luck", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（转折 However，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词\"代表\"→represent；增补（always）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"好运\"见 新华社英文（good luck），S1。", "rp": "`not always`（L2·PC）；`represent …`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "因为从前死者的名字常用红色书写，用红墨水书写中国人名被看成是一种冒犯行为。", "en": "Because the names of the dead used to be written in red, writing a Chinese name in red ink is regarded as an offense.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题A", "map": "红色", "set": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "src": "CET4 2016·12·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=②;①→原因状语从句(Because…)|原因;逻辑:因果→because;时锚:从前→一般过去时;话题:受事→被动(→writing Chinese names in red ink is regarded as…);文化:红墨水→red ink；冒犯行为→an offense", "ta": "受事→被动（is regarded as，CL-04）；意合→形合（因果 because，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"被看成\"→be regarded as / is seen as）；增补（used to）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）。", "rp": "`be regarded as`（L2·PC）；`used to V`（L2·PC）。"}], "examsB": [{"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中国人自古以来就在中秋时节庆祝丰收。", "en": "Since ancient times, Chinese people have celebrated a good harvest during the Mid-Autumn season.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:自古以来→现在完成时|一般现在时;话题:补主语(→Chinese people);文化:中秋时节→the Mid-Autumn season(承中秋节 Mid-Autumn Festival)；丰收→S1 a good harvest(China Daily HK)", "ta": "补主语（话题→主语，Li & Thompson 1976）；时间状语前置（连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "介词化（\"在…时节\"→during…）；增补（a good）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"丰收\"对应官方短语 a good harvest 见 China Daily（chinadaily.com.cn），S1。", "rp": "`since ancient times`（L2·PC）；`celebrate a good harvest`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "这与北美地区庆祝感恩节的习俗十分相似。", "en": "This is very similar to the custom of celebrating Thanksgiving in North America.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→This / This custom);文化:北美→North America；感恩节→Thanksgiving(S4 未定位官方同形)；相似→be similar to", "ta": "补主语（This 回指，Li & Thompson 1976）；意合→形合。", "te": "词性转换（\"习俗\"→the custom of）；增补（very）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"感恩节\"库内未定位官方同形英文，标 S4。", "rp": "`be similar to`（L2·PC）；`the custom of V-ing`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "过中秋节的习俗于唐代早期在中国各地开始流行。", "en": "The custom of celebrating the Mid-Autumn Festival began to gain popularity across China in the early Tang Dynasty.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;时锚:于唐代早期→一般过去时;话题:补主语(→the custom);文化:中秋节→S1 the Mid-Autumn Festival(China Daily/en.chinaculture，定译)；唐代→S1 the Tang Dynasty(China Daily Asia)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合；时间状语后置（连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词短语（\"开始流行\"→began to gain popularity）；词性转换（\"过…的习俗\"→the custom of celebrating…）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"中秋节\"官方定译 the Mid-Autumn Festival，S1；\"唐代\"见 China Daily Asia（the Tang Dynasty），S1。", "rp": "`gain popularity`（L2·PC）；`across China`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "中秋节在农历八月十五，是人们拜月的节日。", "en": "The Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the fifteenth day of the eighth lunar month and is a festival for people to worship the moon.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①\"中秋节在…\"/②\"是…节日\";核心=①;②→并列(and)|同位语;话题:补主语(→the Mid-Autumn Festival);文化:农历→S1 the lunar calendar(中国网/en.chinaculture)；拜月→S1 worship the moon(en.chinaculture)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；定语化（\"拜月的\"→for people to worship the moon）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"在…\"→falls on）；增补（for people）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"农历\"见 中国网/en.chinaculture（the lunar calendar），S1；\"拜月\"见 en.chinaculture（worship the moon），S1。", "rp": "`fall on`（L2·PC）；`a festival for sb to V`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "这天夜晚皓月当空，人们合家团聚，共赏明月。", "en": "On this night, with the bright moon hanging in the sky, families gather for a reunion and appreciate the round moon together.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③;核心=②;①→时间状语(On this night…)/伴随;③→伴随状语(分词)/目的;话题:补主语(→families);文化:皓月当空→S4 the bright moon hangs in the sky；合家团聚→S1 family reunions(新华·央视)；赏月→S1 appreciate the round moon(China Daily)", "ta": "补主语；伴随状语化（with＋独立主格，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（and）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"皓月当空\"→the bright moon hanging in the sky）；动词\"团聚/赏\"→gather / appreciate。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"赏月\"见 China Daily（appreciate the round moon），S1；\"家庭团聚\"见 新华·央视（family reunions），S1；\"皓月当空\"未定位官方同形，标 S4。", "rp": "`with … V-ing`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：with the bright moon hanging in the sky；`gather for a reunion`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "2006年，中秋节被列为中国的文化遗产，2008年又被定为公共假日。", "en": "In 2006, the Mid-Autumn Festival was listed as a form of China's cultural heritage, and in 2008 it was designated as a public holiday.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=并列;逻辑:并列→and;话题:受事→被动(→the Mid-Autumn Festival was listed / was designated);时锚:2006年/2008年→一般过去时;文化:中秋节→S1 the Mid-Autumn Festival；文化遗产→S1 cultural heritage(中国政府网英文版)；公共假日→S1 a public holiday(China Daily)", "ta": "受事→被动（was listed / was designated，CL-04）；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；补主语。", "te": "增补（a form of）；动词\"被列为/定为\"→was listed as / was designated as。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"文化遗产\"见 中国政府网英文版（cultural heritage），S1；\"公共假日\"见 China Daily（a public holiday），S1。", "rp": "`be listed as`（L2·PC）；`be designated as`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "月饼被视为中秋节不可或缺的美食。", "en": "Mooncakes are regarded as an indispensable delicacy of the Mid-Autumn Festival.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:受事→被动(→mooncakes are regarded as);文化:月饼→S1 mooncakes(China Daily，定译)；不可或缺→S4 indispensable", "ta": "受事→被动（are regarded as，CL-04）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"被视为\"→be regarded as）；词性转换（\"美食\"→delicacy）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"月饼\"官方定译 mooncakes，S1；\"不可或缺\"未定位官方同形，标 S4。", "rp": "`be regarded as`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "人们将月饼作为礼物馈赠亲友或在家庭聚会上享用。", "en": "People give mooncakes as gifts to relatives and friends, or enjoy them at family gatherings.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=并列;逻辑:并列→and|or;话题:补主语(→people);文化:月饼→S1 mooncakes；亲友→relatives and friends；家庭聚会→S4 family gatherings", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（or 并列，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词\"馈赠/享用\"→give / enjoy；增补（as gifts）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"月饼\"官方定译 mooncakes，S1；\"家庭聚会\"未定位官方同形，标 S4。", "rp": "`give sth as a gift`（L2·PC）；`at family gatherings`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "传统的月饼上带有“寿”（longevity）、“福”或“和”等字样。", "en": "Traditional mooncakes bear characters such as \"shou\" (longevity), \"fu\" (good fortune) and \"he\" (harmony).", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "中秋节（赏月·拜月·月饼）", "set": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "src": "CET6 2013·12·S3", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:补主语(→traditional mooncakes);文化:月饼→S1 mooncakes；\"寿\"→longevity(原卷括注，同源优先引用)；\"福\"→fu|good fortune；\"和\"→he|harmony；字样→characters", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合。", "te": "惯用等值（\"带有…字样\"→bear characters such as）；同源引用（longevity 取原卷括注）；音译（shou / fu / he）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"月饼\"官方定译 mooncakes，S1；\"寿\"longevity 取原卷括注（同源优先），\"福\"\"和\"按语境通译。", "rp": "`bear the characters of`（L2·PC）；`such as`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "贴春联(Spring Festival couplets)是中国人欢度春节的一个重要习俗。", "en": "Pasting Spring Festival couplets is an important custom for Chinese people to celebrate the Spring Festival.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:动名词主语(→Pasting Spring Festival couplets)(CL-02);文化:春联→S1 Spring Festival couplets(原卷括注，同源优先；China Daily Asia)；春节→S1 the Spring Festival(定译)", "ta": "动名词主语（\"贴春联\"→Pasting…，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "同源引用（Spring Festival couplets）；增补（for Chinese people）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春联\"从原卷括注 Spring Festival couplets（China Daily Asia），S1；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1。", "rp": "`an important custom for sb to V`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "春联由一对诗句和四字横批(horizontal scroll)组成，诗句和横批用金色或黑色写在红纸上，红色代表幸运，金色代表财富。", "en": "Spring Festival couplets are composed of a pair of poetic lines and a four-character horizontal scroll, which are written on red paper in gold or black; red represents good luck and gold represents wealth.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②③④;核心=①;②→定语从句(which are written…);③④→并列(and);逻辑:并列→and;话题:受事→被动(→Spring Festival couplets are composed of…);文化:春联→S1 Spring Festival couplets；横批→horizontal scroll(原卷括注，同源优先)；幸运→luck|good luck；财富→wealth", "ta": "受事→被动（are composed of，CL-04）；定语从句（which…，连淑能 1993）；意合→形合（分号＋and）。", "te": "同源引用（horizontal scroll）；词性转换（\"四字\"→four-character）；增补（poetic）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春联\"从原卷括注 Spring Festival couplets，S1；\"横批\"从原卷括注 horizontal scroll（同源优先）。", "rp": "`be composed of`（L2·PC）；`which are written on …`（L2·SB）→ 本句实例：which are written on red paper in gold or black。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "春联贴在大门左右两侧和门框上方。", "en": "Spring Festival couplets are pasted on both sides of the front gate and above the door frame.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:单分句;核心=①;话题:受事→被动(→Spring Festival couplets are pasted…)|补主语;文化:春联→S1 Spring Festival couplets；大门→the front gate|door", "ta": "受事→被动（are pasted，CL-04）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"左右两侧\"→both sides of）；增补（the front）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春联\"从原卷括注 Spring Festival couplets，S1。", "rp": "`on both sides of`（L2·PC）；`above the door frame`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "春联的诗句体现中国传统诗词的特点，两句诗的字数相同、内容相关。", "en": "The poetic lines of Spring Festival couplets reflect the characteristics of traditional Chinese poetry: the two lines have the same number of characters and are related in content.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列/补充(and);逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→the poetic lines of…);文化:春联→S1 Spring Festival couplets；传统诗词→traditional poetry|classical poetry", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（冒号说明＋and，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "动词\"体现\"→reflect；增补（the same）；词性转换（\"字数\"→the number of characters）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春联\"从原卷括注 Spring Festival couplets，S1。", "rp": "`reflect the characteristics of`（L2·PC）；`be related in content`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "横批凸显春联的主题，更是锦上添花。", "en": "The horizontal scroll highlights the theme of the couplets and even adds a finishing touch.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→递进(and even…);逻辑:递进→and even|which;话题:补主语(→the horizontal scroll);文化:横批→horizontal scroll(承前括注)；锦上添花→S4 add a finishing touch(无官方定译)", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（递进 and even）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"锦上添花\"→adds a finishing touch）；动词\"凸显\"→highlights。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"横批\"承前取 horizontal scroll（原卷括注，同源）；\"锦上添花\"为成语，无官方定译，标 S4 通译。", "rp": "`highlight the theme of`（L2·PC）；`add a finishing touch`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "春联以简洁的文字描绘生动的形象，抒发美好的愿望。", "en": "Spring Festival couplets use concise words to depict vivid images and express beautiful wishes.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=①;②→并列(and);逻辑:并列→and;话题:补主语(→Spring Festival couplets);文化:春联→S1 Spring Festival couplets", "ta": "补主语；意合→形合（and，连淑能 1993）；方式状语化（\"以…描绘\"→use … to depict）。", "te": "动词并列（\"描绘/抒发\"→depict / express）；词性转换（\"愿望\"→wishes）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春联\"从原卷括注 Spring Festival couplets，S1。", "rp": "`use … to V`（L2·PC）；`express wishes`（L2·PC）。"}, {"term": "", "zh": "", "cn": "当家家户户贴春联时，人们就会意识到春节已经正式拉开序幕。", "en": "When every household pastes Spring Festival couplets, people realize that the Spring Festival has officially begun.", "ptype": "真题", "cat": "真题B", "map": "贴春联（含横批）", "set": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "src": "CET6 2022·09·S1", "cls": "Chapter 7", "tip": "断句:分句①②;核心=②;①→时间状语从句(When…);逻辑:时间→when;时锚:已经→现在完成时;话题:补主语(→people);文化:春联→S1 Spring Festival couplets；春节→S1 the Spring Festival(定译)；拉开序幕→S4 mark the official start of", "ta": "补主语；时间状语从句化（When…，连淑能 1993）；时态呼应（现在完成时，连淑能 1993）。", "te": "惯用等值（\"拉开序幕\"→has officially begun / mark the start of）；增补（officially）。", "srcEn": "S2 · 整句无官方译文（整理编译）；\"春联\"从原卷括注 Spring Festival couplets，S1；\"春节\"官方定译 the Spring Festival，S1。", "rp": "`every household`（L2·PC）；`mark the start of`（L2·PC）。"}], "exercises": [{"sec": "Comprehension 理解 · Table Completion 表格填空", "kind": "table", "intro": "Fill in the following table with the information you learned from the reading text.", "table": {"head": ["Traditional Festivals 传统节日", "Activities 活动", "Traditional Food 传统食物"], "rows": [["Spring Festival", "having a family reunion dinner, setting off firecrackers, giving red envelopes, pasting spring couplets, watching the Spring Festival Gala, and visiting temple fairs", "jiaozi and niangao"], ["Lantern Festival", "enjoying lanterns and guessing lantern riddles", "tangyuan or yuanxiao"], ["Qingming Festival", "tomb sweeping and spring outing", "qingtuan, cold porridge, and cold pancakes"], ["Dragon Boat Festival", "racing dragon boat, hanging mugwort and calamus, and wearing scented sachets", "zongzi and realgar wine"], ["Mid-Autumn Festival", "moon appreciation and family gathering", "mooncakes, osmanthus‑flavoured cakes, and osmanthus wine"]]}, "src": ""}, {"sec": "Comprehension 理解 · Matching 连线搭配", "kind": "table", "intro": "Match the ethnic groups with their traditional festivals.", "table": {"head": ["Ethnic Group 民族", "Traditional Festival 传统节日"], "rows": [["the Zhuang ethnic group", "Gexu Singing Festival"], ["the Miao ethnic group", "Flower-Jumping Festival"], ["the Dai ethnic group", "Water-Splashing Festival"], ["the Mongolian ethnic group", "Nadam Fair"], ["the Bai ethnic group", "Third Month Fair"]]}, "src": "课文 u3_s6_p1/u3_s8_p1 等（答案册与原表完全重复=无有效答案，改以课文为准）"}, {"sec": "Translation 翻译 · Term Translation 术语翻译", "kind": "fill", "intro": "", "qs": [{"no": 1, "q": "小年", "ans": "Xiaonian Festival", "src": "课文 u2_s3_p2：The timing of the Xiaonian Festival varies across China."}, {"no": 2, "q": "春节联欢晚会", "ans": "Spring Festival Gala", "src": "课文 u2_s3_p6：Since 1983, watching the Spring Festival Gala has become an integral part of Chinese New Year celebrations for Chinese people both at home and abroad."}, {"no": 3, "q": "二十四节气", "ans": "Twenty-Four Solar Terms", "src": "课文 u2_s4_p5：the Twenty-Four Solar Terms: China's knowledge system and social practice formed through observations of the sun's annual motion, and cognition of the year's changes in season, climate, and phenology."}, {"no": 4, "q": "腊八粥", "ans": "laba porridge", "src": "课文 u2_s3_p1：People eat laba porridge on the day to give thanks for a good harvest and to wish for good luck and health in the coming year."}, {"no": 5, "q": "七夕节", "ans": "Double Seventh Festival", "src": "课文 u2_s10_p1：The Double Seventh Festival, celebrated on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, is a traditional festival full of romance."}, {"no": 6, "q": "月饼", "ans": "mooncake", "src": "课文 u2_s11_p6：Most commonly enjoyed during the Mid-Autumn Festival, mooncakes are traditional Chinese pastries that symbolise reunion and harmony."}]}, {"sec": "Translation 翻译 · Passage Translation 段落翻译", "kind": "passage", "intro": "", "cn": "2024年，联合国教科文组织（UNESCO）将\"春节——中国人庆祝传统新年的社会实践\"列入人类非物质文化遗产代表作名录。与春节相关的传统知识与习俗既通过家庭和社区的非正式传承，又通过教育体系的正式教育得以延续。与春节密切相关的手工技艺和艺术技能通过师徒相承的方式不断传播，弘扬了家庭价值观，促进了社会凝聚与和平，同时赋予人们深厚的文化认同感。", "ans": "In 2024, UNESCO inscribed the Spring Festival, social practices of the Chinese people in celebration of the traditional new year, on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. The traditional knowledge and customs associated with the Spring Festival are passed down informally within families and communities, as well as formally through the education system. Craftsmanship and artistic skills closely related to the festival are transmitted through apprenticeships, promoting family values, social cohesion, and peace, while providing a sense of cultural identity.", "src": ""}, {"sec": "Critical Thinking and Discussion 思辨与讨论", "kind": "open", "intro": "", "qs": [{"no": 1, "q": "Traditional Chinese festivals such as the Spring Festival, the Mid-Autumn Festival, and the Dragon Boat Festival are rooted in history, family values, and culture. Today, many of these celebrations have taken on modern features, such as digital red envelopes, online gifts, virtual reunions, and e-commerce events. Discuss with your partner whether you think that modernising these traditional festivals is necessary to keep them relevant for young people, or if it risks losing their true cultural meaning. Explain your opinions and support them with examples.", "ans": "", "src": ""}]}, {"sec": "Communication 口语任务", "kind": "open", "intro": "", "qs": [{"no": 1, "q": "Valentine's Day in Western countries is a festival of romance, and people give cards, letters, flowers, or presents to their spouse or partner. In China, on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month, people celebrate the Double Seventh Festival, which is a traditional festival regarded as Chinese Valentine's Day. You have a foreign friend who shows great interest in the origin of the festival and how people celebrate it. Try to gather as much information as possible about this traditional festival and explain its origin and customs to your friend.", "ans": "", "src": ""}]}], "vocabHF": []}